(window.STEP_BOOK = window.STEP_BOOK || {})["R5"] = {"n":5,"level":"B2+","t":"Mastering Full STEP Academic Passages","lessons":[{"id":"5.1","t":"Full STEP Academic Passages with Mixed Question Sets","html":"<p><strong>Unit 5 · Lesson 5.1 · CEFR B2+ · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Apply all reading strategies to 450–650-word academic passages.</p>\n<p>2. Choose the right strategy for each question type in a mixed set.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يطبّق الطالب جميع استراتيجيات القراءة على نصوص أكاديمية من 450 إلى 650 كلمة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يختار الاستراتيجية المناسبة لكل نوع سؤال في مجموعة متنوعة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>The longest STEP passages are academic texts of 450 words or more, followed by ten questions of mixed types. At B2+ level, the passages use more abstract vocabulary, longer sentences with several clauses and more subtle relationships between ideas.</p>\n<p>Reading every word slowly is not possible in the time available. Successful candidates read actively: they skim to build a map of the passage (what each paragraph does), then scan precisely for each question. They also recognize the question type immediately and apply the matching strategy.</p>\n<p>In this lesson, each passage is a full STEP-style set. Before answering, write a one-line map: P1 = …, P2 = …, P3 = … This map will save you time on main-idea, organization and NOT/EXCEPT questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ The passage is mainly concerned with …</p>\n<p>▸ According to paragraph 3, …</p>\n<p>▸ The word … in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …</p>\n<p>▸ It can be inferred that the author …</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following is NOT …?</p>\n<p>▸ The phrase … refers to …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أطول نصوص اختبار STEP نصوص أكاديمية من 450 كلمة أو أكثر يتبعها عشرة أسئلة متنوعة، وفي مستوى B2+ تكون المفردات أكثر تجريدًا والجمل أطول والعلاقات بين الأفكار أدق. لذلك اقرأ قراءة نشطة: امسح النص لترسم خريطة لوظيفة كل فقرة، ثم ابحث بدقة لكل سؤال، وتعرّف على نوع السؤال فورًا لتطبق استراتيجيته.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Skim (60–90 seconds): read the title, the first sentence of each paragraph and the last paragraph.</p>\n<p>2. Write a one-line map of the passage in the margin (P1 problem, P2 cause …).</p>\n<p>3. Read each question and label its type before reading the options.</p>\n<p>4. Go to the exact paragraph; read the sentence before and after the key information.</p>\n<p>5. Eliminate wrong options actively; cross them out.</p>\n<p>6. Answer whole-passage questions last, using your map.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">امسح النص في دقيقة إلى دقيقة ونصف، واكتب خريطة من سطر واحد لوظائف الفقرات، وحدد نوع كل سؤال قبل قراءة البدائل، واذهب إلى الفقرة الدقيقة، واستبعد البدائل الخاطئة بشطبها، وأجب عن أسئلة النص الكامل في النهاية مستعينًا بالخريطة.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Getting lost in long sentences — find the main subject and verb first.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an option with the right words in the wrong relationship.</p>\n<p>✗ Over-reading inference questions and choosing a big leap.</p>\n<p>✗ Spending more than two minutes on one question.</p>\n<p>✗ Confusing a detail from one paragraph with the main idea of the passage.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: The Mpemba Effect</strong></p>\n<p><em>(65 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> In 1963, a Tanzanian schoolboy named Erasto Mpemba noticed that hot ice-cream mixture froze faster than cold mixture. His teacher dismissed the observation, but a visiting physicist later tested it and published the results. Since then, the so-called Mpemba effect has been reproduced in some experiments and not in others, and physicists still disagree about whether it is real and, if so, what causes it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The passage is mainly concerned with …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. how ice cream is made in Tanzania</p>\n<p>B. a puzzling observation that scientists still debate</p>\n<p>C. the career of a famous physicist</p>\n<p>D. why teachers ignore students</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Map: observation → test → disagreement.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Only B covers the whole passage.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The word dismissed is closest in meaning to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. rejected as unimportant B. tested carefully</p>\n<p>C. published D. repeated</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Contrast with but a visiting physicist later tested it.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The teacher did not take it seriously.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ارسم خريطة: ملاحظة ← اختبار ← خلاف، ثم اختر الفكرة الشاملة، واستعن بالتضاد but لمعنى dismissed.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Antibiotic resistance","(491 words)","[1] When penicillin was introduced in the 1940s, it was hailed as a miracle drug. For the first time in history, doctors could reliably cure infections that had previously killed millions, from pneumonia to infected wounds. Within a few decades, dozens of new antibiotics had been discovered, and many experts believed that infectious diseases would soon be defeated. Yet in his Nobel Prize speech in 1945, Alexander Fleming, the discoverer of penicillin, had already issued a warning: bacteria could learn to resist the drug if it were used carelessly.","[2] His warning has proved remarkably accurate. Antibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria change in ways that allow them to survive drugs designed to kill them. This happens through natural selection. In any large population of bacteria, a few individuals may carry random genetic mutations that make them less vulnerable to a particular antibiotic. When the drug is used, the susceptible bacteria die, but the resistant ones survive and multiply, eventually replacing the original population. Bacteria can also pass resistance genes directly to one another, even between different species, which allows resistance to spread with alarming speed.","[3] Human behaviour has greatly accelerated this process. Antibiotics are frequently prescribed for viral infections, such as colds and influenza, against which they are completely ineffective. Patients often stop taking their medicine as soon as they feel better, leaving the most resistant bacteria alive. In many countries, antibiotics can still be bought without a prescription. Agriculture is another major factor: in some parts of the world, large quantities of antibiotics are given to healthy farm animals simply to make them grow faster.","[4] The consequences are already serious. The World Health Organization considers antimicrobial resistance one of the top global threats to public health. A major study published in The Lancet estimated that, in 2019, bacterial infections resistant to antibiotics were directly responsible for more than 1.2 million deaths worldwide. Some strains, sometimes called superbugs, are resistant to almost all available drugs. If the trend continues, routine operations such as hip replacements and caesarean sections, as well as cancer chemotherapy, could become far more dangerous, because they depend on effective antibiotics to prevent infection.","[5] Meanwhile, the development of new antibiotics has slowed dramatically. Creating a new drug can take more than ten years and cost over a billion dollars, but because new antibiotics are kept in reserve and used only when older ones fail, pharmaceutical companies earn relatively little from them. As a result, several large companies have abandoned antibiotic research altogether.","[6] Solutions will require action on many levels. Doctors are being encouraged to prescribe antibiotics only when necessary, and rapid diagnostic tests can help them identify whether an infection is bacterial. Governments in several countries, including Saudi Arabia, have strengthened rules requiring prescriptions. Researchers are exploring alternatives, such as viruses that infect and kill specific bacteria. Ultimately, however, experts agree that preserving the effectiveness of existing antibiotics depends on everyone treating them as a precious and limited resource."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The passage is mainly concerned with …","o":["the life of Alexander Fleming","how penicillin was discovered","the causes, effects and possible solutions of antibiotic resistance","the use of antibiotics in agriculture only"],"k":2,"e":"[main idea] Map: history → mechanism → human causes → consequences → slow development → solutions.","h":"الموضوع العام."},{"n":2,"s":"Why does the author mention Fleming's Nobel Prize speech?","o":["to praise Fleming's speaking skills","to explain how penicillin works","to criticize the Nobel Prize","to show that the danger of resistance was predicted early"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] had already issued a warning … His warning has proved remarkably accurate.","h":"التحذير المبكر."},{"n":3,"s":"The word vulnerable in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["strong","common","easily harmed","dangerous"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] mutations that make them less vulnerable to an antibiotic.","h":"عرضة للأذى."},{"n":4,"s":"According to paragraph 2, resistance spreads quickly partly because bacteria …","o":["can pass resistance genes to one another","live for many years","are larger than viruses","cannot change genetically"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Bacteria can also pass resistance genes directly to one another.","h":"انتقال الجينات."},{"n":5,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as human behaviours that accelerate resistance EXCEPT …","o":["washing hands too often","prescribing antibiotics for viral infections","stopping medicine early","giving antibiotics to healthy farm animals"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] viral ✓ stopping ✓ animals ✓ no prescription ✓; handwashing ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":6,"s":"The word they in paragraph 4 (because they depend on effective antibiotics) refers to …","o":["superbugs","antibiotics","routine operations and chemotherapy","drugs companies"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] routine operations … as well as chemotherapy … because they depend on …","h":"العمليات والعلاج."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Because new antibiotics are kept in reserve and used only when older ones fail, pharmaceutical companies earn relatively little from them?","o":["Companies make little profit from new antibiotics, since doctors save them for cases where older drugs no longer work.","New antibiotics are used first, so companies earn a lot.","Companies earn little because new antibiotics are cheap to make.","Older antibiotics are kept in reserve, so companies lose money."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] reserve use → little profit.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":8,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that the decline in research is mainly due to …","o":["a lack of scientists","economic reasons","government bans","the success of older drugs"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] cost over a billion … earn relatively little … abandoned.","h":"أسباب اقتصادية."},{"n":9,"s":"The word abandoned in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["increased","started","hidden","given up"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] abandoned antibiotic research altogether.","h":"تخلّى عن."},{"n":10,"s":"The author would most likely agree that …","o":["new antibiotics will soon solve the problem","resistance only affects poor countries","antibiotics should be used for colds","protecting antibiotics is the responsibility of patients, doctors and governments alike"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] action on many levels … everyone treating them as precious.","h":"يتفق مع."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): Behavioural economics and everyday choices","(456 words)","[1] Traditional economic theory was built on a simple assumption: people are rational. Faced with a choice, the theory said, individuals carefully compare costs and benefits and select the option that best serves their interests. This model, often called homo economicus, made economics elegant and mathematically precise. The trouble is that real people frequently behave in ways the model cannot explain.","[2] Behavioural economics emerged in the 1970s and 1980s to address this gap. Combining insights from psychology and economics, researchers such as Daniel Kahneman, Amos Tversky and Richard Thaler studied how people actually make decisions. Their experiments revealed systematic patterns of irrational behaviour — not random mistakes, but predictable biases shared by most people. Kahneman received the Nobel Prize in economics in 2002, and Thaler in 2017, signalling that the field had moved from the margins to the mainstream.","[3] One of the best-documented biases is loss aversion: losses feel roughly twice as painful as equivalent gains feel pleasant. Losing one hundred riyals upsets us more than finding one hundred riyals pleases us. This helps explain why investors hold on to falling shares for too long, hoping to avoid accepting a loss, and why free trials that later require cancellation are so effective for companies.","[4] Another is the anchoring effect. When people estimate a value, they are strongly influenced by the first number they see, even if it is irrelevant. In one famous experiment, participants spun a wheel that landed on either 10 or 65 and were then asked to estimate the percentage of African countries in the United Nations. Those who saw 65 gave much higher estimates. Shops exploit this tendency when they display an expensive item first, making everything else seem reasonably priced by comparison.","[5] Perhaps the most practical idea to emerge from the field is the nudge. A nudge is a small change in how choices are presented that influences behaviour without forbidding any option or significantly changing financial incentives. For example, when employees are automatically enrolled in a pension savings plan and must actively choose to leave it, participation rates rise dramatically compared with plans that require employees to sign up. Placing fruit at eye level in school cafeterias increases the amount of fruit students choose. Governments in Britain, the United States and elsewhere have created special nudge units to apply these insights to public policy.","[6] Nudges are not without critics. Some argue that they can be manipulative, steering people towards choices that governments or companies prefer without their full awareness. Others point out that small nudges cannot solve large structural problems such as poverty. Supporters respond that every choice is presented in some way, so the question is not whether to influence behaviour but how to do it transparently and in people's own interests."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","o":["to argue that economics is useless","to explain how behavioural economics challenges the idea that people always choose rationally","to describe the life of Daniel Kahneman","to advise readers how to invest"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] rational model → behavioural economics → biases → nudges → criticism.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":12,"s":"The word elegant in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["fashionable","expensive","simple and neat","confusing"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] made economics elegant and mathematically precise.","h":"أنيق / بسيط."},{"n":13,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that the Nobel Prizes given to Kahneman and Thaler …","o":["proved that traditional economics was useless","were given for work in psychology only","showed that behavioural economics had gained wide acceptance","were controversial among all economists"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] signalling that the field had moved from the margins to the mainstream.","h":"القبول الواسع."},{"n":14,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Losses feel roughly twice as painful as equivalent gains feel pleasant?","o":["Gains feel twice as good as losses feel bad.","Losses and gains feel exactly the same.","People never feel pleasure from gains.","Losing something hurts about twice as much as gaining the same thing pleases us."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] loss pain ≈ 2 × gain pleasure.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":15,"s":"Why does the author mention free trials that require cancellation?","o":["as an example of companies benefiting from loss aversion","to warn readers about online shopping","to explain how investors choose shares","to describe the anchoring effect"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] This helps explain … why free trials … are so effective.","h":"مثال."},{"n":16,"s":"In the wheel experiment, the number on the wheel was …","o":["irrelevant to the question but still influenced answers","the correct answer to the question","chosen by the participants","the number of UN countries"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] even if it is irrelevant … Those who saw 65 gave higher estimates.","h":"غير ذي صلة."},{"n":17,"s":"The word exploit in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["avoid","take advantage of","explain","measure"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Shops exploit this tendency.","h":"يستغل."},{"n":18,"s":"According to paragraph 5, a nudge does NOT …","o":["influence behaviour","change how choices are presented","aim to change what people choose","forbid any of the available options"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] without forbidding any option.","h":"ما لا يفعله."},{"n":19,"s":"The phrase this tendency in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["spinning a wheel","buying expensive items","being influenced by the first number seen","joining the United Nations"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] strongly influenced by the first number they see … this tendency.","h":"الإحالة."},{"n":20,"s":"How do supporters respond to the criticism that nudges are manipulative?","o":["They admit nudges should be banned.","They say nudges can solve poverty.","They argue that choices are always presented in some way, so influence should be transparent and helpful.","They deny that nudges influence behaviour."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] every choice is presented in some way … do it transparently.","h":"رد المؤيدين."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Desert agriculture technology","(455 words)","[1] Saudi Arabia is one of the driest countries on Earth. It has no permanent rivers, receives on average less than 100 millimetres of rain a year, and for decades relied heavily on ancient groundwater reserves to irrigate its farms. In the 1980s and 1990s, the Kingdom became a major wheat producer — at one stage even exporting grain — but this success came at a heavy cost. Fossil aquifers that had taken tens of thousands of years to fill were being drained within a few decades. Recognizing that this was unsustainable, the government phased out large-scale wheat cultivation by 2016 and began to rely more on imports.","[2] Today, the challenge is to produce more food with far less water. Several technologies are making this possible. The first is precision irrigation. Instead of flooding fields or using sprinklers that lose much of their water to evaporation, drip systems deliver small amounts of water directly to the roots of each plant. When combined with soil-moisture sensors and weather data, these systems can reduce water use by up to fifty percent while maintaining or even increasing yields.","[3] A second approach is controlled-environment agriculture. In greenhouses equipped with cooling systems and shade screens, farmers can grow tomatoes, cucumbers and peppers throughout the year. More advanced facilities use hydroponics, in which plants grow in nutrient-rich water rather than soil, recycling most of the water they use. Vertical farms, which stack growing layers on top of one another inside warehouses lit by LEDs, take this further, using a fraction of the land and water required by conventional farms. Several such farms have opened in Riyadh and Jeddah in recent years.","[4] Researchers are also looking beyond technology to biology. At King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST), scientists are studying plants that can tolerate salty water and extreme heat, with the aim of developing crops that can be irrigated with brackish groundwater or even diluted seawater. Others are investigating how beneficial soil microbes can help plants survive drought.","[5] These innovations are promising, but they are not a complete solution. Greenhouses and vertical farms consume large amounts of energy for cooling and lighting, and unless that energy comes from renewable sources, their environmental benefits are reduced. High start-up costs also make advanced systems difficult for small farmers to adopt. Moreover, while vegetables and herbs can be grown efficiently indoors, staple crops such as wheat and rice still require vast areas of land.","[6] For these reasons, experts argue that desert agriculture should aim not for complete self-sufficiency but for greater resilience: producing high-value fresh foods locally, protecting remaining groundwater, reducing food waste and maintaining reliable import partnerships. In a warming world, the lessons learned in Saudi Arabia's deserts may become valuable far beyond its borders."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"The passage is mainly concerned with …","o":["the history of wheat exports","how Saudi Arabia is using technology and science to farm with little water","the life of KAUST researchers","how to build a greenhouse"],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] Map: water problem → technologies → biology → limits → resilience.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":22,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that the wheat programme ended mainly because …","o":["it was using up irreplaceable groundwater","farmers no longer wanted to grow wheat","wheat prices rose sharply","the rainfall increased"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Fossil aquifers … drained … unsustainable … phased out.","h":"استنزاف المياه."},{"n":23,"s":"The word unsustainable in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very profitable","easy to repair","popular with farmers","unable to continue for a long time"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Recognizing that this was unsustainable, the government phased out …","h":"غير مستدام."},{"n":24,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence When combined with soil-moisture sensors and weather data, these systems can reduce water use by up to fifty percent while maintaining or even increasing yields?","o":["Sensors always double crop yields.","With sensors and weather information, drip irrigation can cut water use by as much as half without lowering harvests.","Drip systems use fifty percent more water than sprinklers.","Weather data reduces yields by fifty percent."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] up to 50% less water + same or higher yields.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":25,"s":"According to paragraph 3, hydroponics is a method in which plants …","o":["grow in nutrient-rich water instead of soil","are watered with seawater","are grown outdoors in sand","are stacked in fields"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] plants grow in nutrient-rich water rather than soil.","h":"الزراعة المائية."},{"n":26,"s":"The word their in paragraph 5 (their environmental benefits) refers to …","o":["small farmers","greenhouses and vertical farms","renewable sources","staple crops"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] Greenhouses and vertical farms consume … their environmental benefits.","h":"الإحالة."},{"n":27,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as limitations of the new technologies EXCEPT …","o":["high energy use","high start-up costs","they cannot grow vegetables","difficulty growing staple crops indoors"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Vegetables CAN be grown efficiently indoors.","h":"ليس قيدًا."},{"n":28,"s":"Why are scientists at KAUST studying salt-tolerant plants?","o":["to make food taste saltier","to develop crops that can be irrigated with salty water","to grow plants in space","to remove salt from the soil completely"],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] irrigated with brackish groundwater or even diluted seawater.","h":"الهدف."},{"n":29,"s":"The word resilience in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to …","o":["the ability to cope with difficulties","complete independence","a type of crop","high profits"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] not complete self-sufficiency but greater resilience.","h":"المرونة / الصمود."},{"n":30,"s":"The author's attitude towards desert agriculture technology is best described as …","o":["enthusiastic without reservations","completely negative","uninterested","positive but realistic"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] promising, but they are not a complete solution.","h":"إيجابي واقعي."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"5.2","t":"Summary and Best-Conclusion Questions","html":"<p><strong>Unit 5 · Lesson 5.2 · CEFR B2+ · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Choose the best summary of a passage.</p>\n<p>2. Choose the most logical conclusion that follows from the passage.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يختار الطالب أفضل ملخص للنص.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يختار الاستنتاج الختامي الأكثر منطقية الذي يترتب على النص.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>A summary question asks: Which of the following best summarizes the passage? A good summary includes the MAIN points of the whole passage, in balance, without minor details and without new information. Wrong summaries are too narrow (one paragraph), too detailed (numbers and names), distorted (wrong relationship) or include ideas not in the passage.</p>\n<p>A conclusion question asks what logically follows: Which of the following can be concluded from the passage? / Which statement would best conclude the final paragraph? The correct answer is supported by the whole argument and fits the author's attitude. It does not introduce a completely new topic or contradict earlier points.</p>\n<p>Both question types require you to understand the passage as a whole. A one-line map of each paragraph is the best tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following best summarizes the passage?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following can best be concluded from the passage?</p>\n<p>▸ Which sentence would best conclude the last paragraph?</p>\n<p>▸ The author's main conclusion is that …</p>\n<p>▸ Which summary of paragraphs 2–4 is most accurate?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سؤال الملخص يطلب أفضل تلخيص للنص كله: يشمل النقاط الرئيسة بتوازن دون تفاصيل ثانوية أو معلومات جديدة. أما الملخصات الخاطئة فضيقة (فقرة واحدة)، أو مليئة بالتفاصيل، أو مشوهة العلاقة، أو تضيف أفكارًا. وسؤال الخاتمة يطلب ما يترتب منطقيًا على النص ويتوافق مع موقف الكاتب دون موضوع جديد.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Build a paragraph map: one short phrase per paragraph.</p>\n<p>2. Join the phrases into one or two sentences — this is your predicted summary.</p>\n<p>3. Reject options that cover only one or two paragraphs.</p>\n<p>4. Reject options full of specific numbers, names or examples.</p>\n<p>5. For conclusions, check the author's attitude and the direction of the argument.</p>\n<p>6. Reject conclusions that go beyond the evidence (always, all, will certainly).</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ارسم خريطة الفقرات واجمعها في جملة أو جملتين لتتوقع الملخص، واستبعد البدائل التي تغطي فقرة أو فقرتين فقط أو المليئة بالأرقام والأمثلة، وفي الخاتمة تحقق من موقف الكاتب واتجاه الحجة واستبعد المبالغة.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Too narrow: summarizes only the first or last paragraph.</p>\n<p>✗ Too detailed: lists examples instead of ideas.</p>\n<p>✗ Distorted: changes cause and effect or the author's position.</p>\n<p>✗ Beyond the text: a conclusion that is logical in general but not supported here.</p>\n<p>✗ Contradicts the author's attitude.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Libraries of Seeds</strong></p>\n<p><em>(69 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Around the world, seed banks store millions of seeds from crops and wild plants. The largest, the Svalbard Global Seed Vault in Norway, is built deep inside a mountain to protect its samples from disasters. Seed banks allow scientists to recover varieties that are lost through war, disease or climate change. In 2015, for example, researchers withdrew seeds from Svalbard to replace collections damaged by the conflict in Syria.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Which of the following best summarizes the passage?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Svalbard is a mountain in Norway.</p>\n<p>B. Seed banks protect plant varieties so that they can be recovered after losses.</p>\n<p>C. The conflict in Syria damaged seed collections.</p>\n<p>D. Climate change is destroying all plants.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Map: seed banks → largest one → purpose → example.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A and C are details; D is extreme and not stated.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملخص الصحيح يجمع فكرة النص كلها، أما A وC فتفاصيل وD مبالغة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Microplastics in the oceans","(467 words)","[1] Plastic has transformed modern life. It is cheap, light, strong and can be shaped into almost anything, from bottles and packaging to car parts and medical equipment. Global production has risen from about two million tonnes in 1950 to more than 400 million tonnes a year today. Yet the very durability that makes plastic so useful has also made it one of the most persistent pollutants on the planet. Scientists estimate that millions of tonnes of plastic waste enter the oceans every year, carried by rivers, blown from landfill sites or dropped directly by people on beaches and boats.","[2] Much of the plastic that enters the sea does not disappear; it breaks down. Exposed to sunlight, waves and salt water, larger items gradually fragment into smaller and smaller pieces. When these fragments are smaller than five millimetres, they are called microplastics. Other microplastics enter the environment already small: fibres released when synthetic clothes are washed, particles worn from car tyres, and tiny beads that were once added to some cosmetics.","[3] Microplastics have now been found almost everywhere scientists have looked: on remote Arctic ice, in deep-sea sediments, in the stomachs of fish, seabirds and whales, and in table salt, bottled water and honey. They have even been detected in the air of cities and in falling rain. A study of seafood sold in markets found microplastics in a large proportion of shellfish samples. Because shellfish such as mussels and oysters are eaten whole, people who eat them regularly are likely to swallow thousands of particles each year.","[4] The effects on marine life are a growing concern. Laboratory studies show that small animals such as zooplankton can mistake microplastics for food, filling their stomachs with material that provides no nutrition. Plastics can also absorb toxic chemicals from the surrounding water and may carry these substances into the bodies of animals that eat them. Since zooplankton form the base of the ocean food chain, damage at this level could affect many other species.","[5] The risks to human health are less clear. Microplastics have been detected in human blood, lungs and placentas, but scientists do not yet know what concentrations, if any, are harmful. Research is difficult because microplastics vary enormously in size, shape and chemical composition, and because it is almost impossible to find a control group of people who have not been exposed to them.","[6] What is clear is that the amount of microplastics in the environment will continue to increase for decades, even if new pollution stopped today, because larger plastic waste already in the sea will keep breaking down. For this reason, most experts argue that the priority must be to stop plastic reaching the ocean in the first place, through better waste management, reduced single-use packaging, and improved filters in washing machines and water-treatment plants."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes the passage?","o":["Plastic production has grown from two million to 400 million tonnes since 1950.","Shellfish contain microplastics, so people should stop eating mussels.","Microplastics come only from cosmetics and car tyres.","Microplastics are spreading throughout the environment and food chain; their health effects are uncertain, so preventing plastic pollution is the priority."],"k":3,"e":"[summary / conclusion] Map: plastic useful but persistent → microplastics → everywhere → effects → uncertain human risk → prevention.","h":"أفضل ملخص."},{"n":2,"s":"The word persistent in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very colourful","lasting for a long time","easily removed","very light"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] the very durability … one of the most persistent pollutants.","h":"دائم / مستمر."},{"n":3,"s":"According to paragraph 2, all of the following are sources of microplastics EXCEPT …","o":["glass bottles","synthetic clothes","car tyres","cosmetic beads"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] fibres ✓ tyres ✓ beads ✓ fragments ✓; glass ✗.","h":"ليس مصدرًا."},{"n":4,"s":"Why are people who eat shellfish likely to swallow many microplastics?","o":["Shellfish live on Arctic ice.","Shellfish are eaten whole.","Shellfish are cooked in plastic.","Shellfish produce plastic."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] Because shellfish … are eaten whole.","h":"السبب."},{"n":5,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes paragraph 4?","o":["Zooplankton are the largest animals in the sea.","Toxic chemicals come only from plastics.","Laboratory studies are unreliable.","Microplastics may harm marine animals and could affect the whole ocean food chain."],"k":3,"e":"[summary / conclusion] mistake for food; carry chemicals; food chain risk.","h":"ملخص الفقرة."},{"n":6,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that scientists cannot easily compare exposed and unexposed people because …","o":["people refuse to take part in studies","almost everyone has already been exposed to microplastics","microplastics are all the same","blood tests are not possible"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] almost impossible to find a control group … not exposed.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":7,"s":"The author's attitude towards the risks to human health is best described as …","o":["alarmed and certain of serious harm","completely dismissive","cautious, because the evidence is still unclear","humorous"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] The risks to human health are less clear … do not yet know.","h":"حذر."},{"n":8,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the final paragraph?","o":["Microplastic levels will fall immediately if pollution stops.","Reducing new plastic pollution is essential, even though microplastic levels will keep rising for some time.","Nothing can be done about microplastics.","Washing machines are the main cause of ocean pollution."],"k":1,"e":"[claim / evidence] will continue to increase … priority must be to stop plastic reaching the ocean.","h":"الخلاصة."},{"n":9,"s":"The phrase in the first place in paragraph 6 means …","o":["in the most important location","for the first time in history","in first position in a race","from the beginning"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] stop plastic reaching the ocean in the first place.","h":"من الأساس."},{"n":10,"s":"Which sentence would best follow the last sentence of the passage?","o":["Without such measures, the problem will be passed on to future generations.","Plastic was invented in the early twentieth century.","Whales are the largest mammals on Earth.","Honey is produced by bees from flower nectar."],"k":0,"e":"[claim / evidence] A logical conclusion that continues the argument.","h":"الجملة الختامية."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The rise of megacities","(466 words)","[1] In 1950, only two cities in the world, New York and Tokyo, had populations of more than ten million. Today, there are over thirty such megacities, and the United Nations expects the number to keep rising. Most of the growth is taking place not in Europe or North America but in Asia and Africa, in cities such as Delhi, Dhaka, Lagos and Kinshasa. By 2050, around two-thirds of the world's population is expected to live in urban areas. This represents one of the largest movements of people in human history, and it is changing the economic, social and environmental future of entire continents.","[2] Several forces drive this growth. The most important is migration from rural areas, as people leave farming in search of jobs, education and health care. In many countries, agricultural work has become less profitable, while cities offer a wider range of opportunities. Natural population growth within cities adds further pressure. In some regions, conflict and climate-related disasters such as droughts and floods also push people towards urban centres. Once a few members of a family have settled in a city, relatives and neighbours often follow, using the connections they have already made to find housing and work.","[3] Megacities can be powerful engines of economic development. Large populations attract investment, encourage innovation and allow companies to benefit from large markets and pools of skilled workers. Universities, hospitals, cultural institutions and international airports tend to be concentrated in large cities, attracting talented people from across the country and abroad. Concentrating people in cities can also be more efficient: it is cheaper to provide water, electricity, schools and hospitals to people living close together than to the same number of people spread across the countryside.","[4] However, rapid growth often outpaces planning. In many megacities, a large share of residents live in informal settlements without secure housing, clean water or proper sanitation. Traffic congestion wastes millions of hours and contributes to air pollution, which is linked to respiratory diseases. Long daily commutes also reduce the time people can spend with their families. Many coastal megacities, including Jakarta, Manila and Lagos, are also vulnerable to rising sea levels, and parts of Jakarta are sinking because so much groundwater has been extracted.","[5] Some cities are responding with ambitious plans. Investment in mass transit, such as metro systems and dedicated bus lanes, can reduce congestion. Upgrading informal settlements, rather than demolishing them, can improve living conditions while preserving communities. Indonesia has gone further, beginning construction of a new capital on the island of Borneo, partly to relieve pressure on Jakarta.","[6] The future of megacities will depend largely on governance — the ability of governments to plan, finance and manage growth fairly. Well-governed megacities can offer their residents prosperity and opportunity; poorly managed ones risk becoming places of inequality and environmental stress."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes the passage?","o":["Delhi and Lagos are the largest cities in the world.","Jakarta is sinking because of groundwater extraction.","Megacities are growing rapidly, bringing economic benefits and serious problems, and their future depends on good management.","Migration from rural areas should be stopped."],"k":2,"e":"[summary / conclusion] Map: growth → causes → benefits → problems → responses → governance.","h":"أفضل ملخص."},{"n":12,"s":"According to paragraph 1, where is most megacity growth taking place?","o":["in Asia and Africa","in Europe","in North America","in Australia"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Most of the growth is taking place … in Asia and Africa.","h":"آسيا وأفريقيا."},{"n":13,"s":"All of the following are given as causes of megacity growth EXCEPT …","o":["lower housing costs in cities","migration from rural areas","natural population growth","conflict and climate disasters"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] migration ✓ natural growth ✓ conflict/climate ✓; cheap housing ✗.","h":"ليس سببًا."},{"n":14,"s":"The word engines in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["machines in cars","train stations","factories","powerful forces that drive something"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] powerful engines of economic development.","h":"محركات (قوى دافعة)."},{"n":15,"s":"Why does the author say concentrating people in cities can be more efficient?","o":["Services are cheaper to provide when people live close together.","People in cities work longer hours.","Cities have more farmland.","Rural people do not need services."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] cheaper to provide water, electricity … to people living close together.","h":"الكفاءة."},{"n":16,"s":"The word outpaces in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["follows","supports","stops","moves faster than"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] rapid growth often outpaces planning.","h":"يتجاوز في السرعة."},{"n":17,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes paragraph 4?","o":["Jakarta is the most polluted city in the world.","Fast growth causes problems such as poor housing, traffic, pollution and flooding risks.","Informal settlements always have clean water.","Traffic in megacities is easy to solve."],"k":1,"e":"[summary / conclusion] informal settlements, congestion, pollution, sea levels.","h":"ملخص الفقرة."},{"n":18,"s":"Why does the author mention Indonesia's new capital?","o":["as an example of a major response to megacity pressure","to describe tourism in Borneo","to criticize the government of Indonesia","to explain why Jakarta is popular"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] Indonesia has gone further … partly to relieve pressure on Jakarta.","h":"مثال."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the passage?","o":["All megacities will become places of inequality.","Megacities should be broken into small towns.","Whether megacities succeed or fail depends mainly on how well they are governed.","Rural life is always better than city life."],"k":2,"e":"[claim / evidence] The future … will depend largely on governance.","h":"الخلاصة."},{"n":20,"s":"The author's attitude towards megacities is best described as …","o":["entirely optimistic","balanced, recognizing both opportunities and risks","entirely pessimistic","indifferent"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] engines of development … However, rapid growth …","h":"متوازن."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Citizen science projects","(454 words)","[1] For most of history, scientific research was the work of a small group of professionals in universities and laboratories. Today, millions of ordinary people around the world contribute to real scientific projects, often from their own homes or neighbourhoods. This approach, known as citizen science, is helping researchers collect and analyse data on a scale that would otherwise be impossible. Participants range from schoolchildren and retired people to farmers, fishermen and amateur astronomers, and most of them have no formal scientific training.","[2] Citizen science is not entirely new. For more than a century, volunteers in North America have counted birds every December in an event called the Christmas Bird Count, producing one of the longest-running wildlife records in the world. What has changed is technology. Smartphones with cameras and GPS, together with online platforms, now allow anyone to record observations, upload photographs and share them instantly with scientists. Apps can even suggest the name of a species from a photograph, helping beginners make accurate identifications and learn as they go.","[3] Projects take many forms. In some, volunteers act as observers in the field, recording plants, insects or weather conditions. In others, they help analyse data online. On the platform Zooniverse, for example, volunteers have classified hundreds of thousands of galaxies from telescope images, transcribed historical documents and identified animals in camera-trap photographs. In 2007, a Dutch schoolteacher taking part in such a project noticed a strange green object in a telescope image; it turned out to be a previously unknown type of astronomical object, now known as Hanny's Voorwerp.","[4] The benefits extend beyond data. Participants often report that they learn about science, develop new skills and feel more connected to nature or to their communities. Some projects focus on local problems, such as measuring air quality near schools or mapping litter on beaches, giving residents evidence they can use to campaign for change. In several cities, data collected by volunteers has persuaded local authorities to install new monitoring stations or to change traffic routes near schools.","[5] Nevertheless, citizen science raises legitimate questions about data quality. Volunteers may misidentify species or record observations inconsistently, and they tend to collect more data near cities and roads than in remote areas. Researchers address these problems by providing training, asking several volunteers to classify the same item, and using statistical methods to correct for uneven sampling. Studies comparing volunteer and expert data have generally found that, with such safeguards, citizen science data can be highly reliable.","[6] As environmental challenges grow and research budgets remain limited, citizen science seems likely to play an even larger role in the future. In Saudi Arabia, for instance, volunteers have helped record bird migration and monitor coral reefs along the Red Sea coast."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes the passage?","o":["A Dutch teacher discovered Hanny's Voorwerp in 2007.","The Christmas Bird Count is the oldest wildlife record in the world.","Citizen science lets the public contribute to research; it has many benefits and, with safeguards, produces reliable data.","Citizen science data is always less accurate than expert data."],"k":2,"e":"[summary / conclusion] Map: definition → history/technology → forms → benefits → data quality → future.","h":"أفضل ملخص."},{"n":22,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what has mainly changed in citizen science?","o":["the number of birds","the interest of scientists","the technology that volunteers use","the season of the bird count"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] What has changed is technology.","h":"التقنية."},{"n":23,"s":"Why does the author mention the Dutch schoolteacher?","o":["to show that volunteers can make real discoveries","to criticize professional astronomers","to explain how telescopes work","to describe teaching in the Netherlands"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] noticed … a previously unknown type of astronomical object.","h":"مثال على الاكتشاف."},{"n":24,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as tasks done by volunteers on Zooniverse EXCEPT …","o":["classifying galaxies","transcribing historical documents","identifying animals in photographs","building telescopes"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] galaxies ✓ documents ✓ animals ✓; building telescopes ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes paragraph 4?","o":["Volunteers are paid for measuring air quality.","Beaches are full of litter.","Citizen science benefits participants and can help communities address local problems.","Only scientists benefit from citizen science."],"k":2,"e":"[summary / conclusion] learn, skills, connection; local problems; evidence for change.","h":"ملخص الفقرة."},{"n":26,"s":"The word legitimate in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["illegal","unimportant","new","reasonable and justified"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] raises legitimate questions about data quality.","h":"مشروع / مبرر."},{"n":27,"s":"It can be inferred that volunteers collect more data near cities because …","o":["there are more species in cities","remote areas have no animals","scientists forbid remote observations","these areas are easier for people to reach"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] collect more data near cities and roads than in remote areas.","h":"سهولة الوصول."},{"n":28,"s":"The word safeguards in paragraph 5 refers to …","o":["security guards","methods used to protect data quality","computer passwords","dangerous species"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] training, multiple classifications, statistics … with such safeguards.","h":"الضمانات."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the passage?","o":["Professional scientists will no longer be needed.","Citizen science should be limited to birds.","Citizen science is likely to become increasingly important for research.","Volunteers' data should never be used."],"k":2,"e":"[claim / evidence] seems likely to play an even larger role in the future.","h":"الخلاصة."},{"n":30,"s":"The author's attitude towards citizen science is best described as …","o":["completely critical","favourable, while acknowledging its limitations","neutral and uninterested","unrealistically enthusiastic"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] benefits … legitimate questions … highly reliable with safeguards.","h":"مؤيد مع الإقرار بالحدود."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"5.3","t":"Sentence Insertion and Paragraph Function","html":"<p><strong>Unit 5 · Lesson 5.3 · CEFR B2+ · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Decide where an extra sentence fits best.</p>\n<p>2. Identify the function of a paragraph in the whole passage.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحدد الطالب أفضل موضع لإدراج جملة إضافية.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يحدد وظيفة الفقرة في النص كله.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Sentence insertion questions give you a new sentence and ask where it fits best — usually at one of four marked positions, here shown as (i), (ii), (iii) and (iv). The right position is the one where the sentence connects logically to what comes BEFORE and AFTER it.</p>\n<p>Look for connecting clues in the new sentence: reference words (this, these, such, they), contrast or addition words (however, also, moreover, as a result), and repeated key ideas. If the new sentence begins with This discovery, the sentence before it must describe a discovery.</p>\n<p>Paragraph function questions ask why a paragraph is there: to introduce the topic, to give background, to provide an example, to present a counter-argument, to explain a cause, to describe a consequence, or to conclude. Think about the paragraph's relationship to the paragraphs before and after it.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Where would the following sentence best fit in paragraph 3?</p>\n<p>▸ In which position, (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv), does the sentence best fit?</p>\n<p>▸ What is the function of paragraph 2?</p>\n<p>▸ Paragraph 4 mainly serves to …</p>\n<p>▸ Why does the author include paragraph 5?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في أسئلة إدراج الجملة تُعطى جملة جديدة وتختار أفضل موضع لها، وهو الموضع الذي ترتبط فيه منطقيًا بما قبلها وما بعدها. ابحث عن قرائن الربط في الجملة الجديدة: كلمات الإحالة (this، these، such) وأدوات التضاد والإضافة (however، also، as a result) وتكرار الفكرة. أما أسئلة وظيفة الفقرة فتسأل لماذا وُضعت الفقرة: للتقديم، أو الخلفية، أو المثال، أو الرأي المعارض، أو السبب، أو النتيجة، أو الخاتمة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Read the new sentence and underline its connecting words (this, however, also, such, they).</p>\n<p>2. Ask: What MUST come before this sentence? What could come after it?</p>\n<p>3. Try the sentence in each position and read the sentences around it.</p>\n<p>4. Reject positions that break a reference chain or separate a statement from its example.</p>\n<p>5. For paragraph function, describe the paragraph's job in two or three words (example, counter-argument, background) before reading the options.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ضع خطًا تحت كلمات الربط في الجملة الجديدة، واسأل: ما الذي يجب أن يسبقها؟ ثم جرّبها في كل موضع، واستبعد الموضع الذي يقطع سلسلة الإحالة أو يفصل الفكرة عن مثالها. وفي وظيفة الفقرة صِف دورها بكلمتين قبل قراءة البدائل.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Choosing a position only because a key word is repeated nearby.</p>\n<p>✗ Ignoring a reference word such as This or These in the new sentence.</p>\n<p>✗ Placing a contrast sentence (However, …) where there is nothing to contrast.</p>\n<p>✗ Describing what a paragraph is about instead of what it does.</p>\n<p>✗ Confusing an example paragraph with a conclusion.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Honey That Never Spoils</strong></p>\n<p><em>(56 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Honey is one of the few foods that can last almost forever. (i) Archaeologists have found pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that were still edible after thousands of years. (ii) This remarkable durability has several causes. (iii) Honey contains very little water, and it is naturally acidic, so bacteria cannot survive in it. (iv)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Where would the following sentence best fit? 'In addition, bees add an enzyme that produces small amounts of hydrogen peroxide, which also kills germs.'</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. (i) B. (ii) C. (iii) D. (iv)</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Clue: In addition … also kills germs → it adds to a list of causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The first cause (little water, acidic) is before (iv).</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is D.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. What is the function of the second sentence (Archaeologists have found …)?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. to give evidence for the first sentence</p>\n<p>B. to introduce a counter-argument</p>\n<p>C. to explain how honey is made</p>\n<p>D. to conclude the paragraph</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Sentence 1 makes a claim; sentence 2 proves it with an example.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبارة In addition تعني أن الجملة تضيف سببًا آخر، فمكانها بعد السبب الأول عند (iv).</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 D · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): The history of mapmaking","(465 words)","[1] Maps are among the oldest tools humans have created to understand their world. Long before writing was invented, people scratched simple plans of hunting grounds and rivers onto stone, bone and clay. A clay tablet from ancient Babylon, made around 600 BC, shows the world as a flat disc surrounded by water, with Babylon at its centre. Such maps were not only practical guides; they also expressed how a society saw itself and its place in the universe. Many early maps placed the mapmaker's own city or holy site at the centre, a habit that continued for thousands of years in different cultures.","[2] The ancient Greeks transformed mapmaking into a science. By observing ships disappearing over the horizon and the shadow of the Earth on the Moon during eclipses, Greek thinkers concluded that the Earth was round. Around 240 BC, Eratosthenes estimated its circumference with surprising accuracy by comparing the angle of the Sun's rays in two Egyptian cities. Later, Ptolemy developed a system of latitude and longitude that allowed places to be located precisely on a grid. His great work, the Geography, listed the coordinates of about 8,000 places and remained influential for more than a thousand years.","[3] Scholars in the Islamic world preserved and greatly extended this knowledge. (i) In the ninth century, the Caliph al-Ma'mun commissioned a team of scholars to measure the Earth and produce an improved world map. (ii) In 1154, the geographer al-Idrisi, working at the court of King Roger II of Sicily, completed one of the most detailed world maps of the Middle Ages, accompanied by a book describing the lands, peoples and trade routes of the known world. (iii) His map, which placed south at the top, was used and copied for centuries. (iv)","[4] The Age of Exploration in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries created both new demands and new problems for mapmakers. Sailors needed charts that would allow them to follow a constant compass direction across oceans. In 1569, the Flemish mapmaker Gerardus Mercator solved this with a projection that showed lines of constant direction as straight lines. However, his method greatly enlarged areas near the poles: on a Mercator map, Greenland appears almost as large as Africa, although Africa is in fact about fourteen times bigger.","[5] In the twentieth century, aerial photography and then satellites revolutionized mapmaking once again. Today, digital maps on our phones combine satellite images, street-level photographs and real-time traffic data, and they update automatically. Yet every map, however advanced, still involves choices about what to include, what to leave out and how to represent a round planet on a flat surface. In that sense, modern maps share something important with the Babylonian clay tablet: they reflect not only the world, but also the priorities of the people who make them."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 1?","o":["to explain how satellites work","to present a counter-argument","to introduce the topic by describing the earliest maps and their meaning","to describe Mercator's projection"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Oldest tools … Babylon … how a society saw itself.","h":"التقديم."},{"n":2,"s":"Where would the following sentence best fit in paragraph 3? 'They calculated the length of one degree of latitude by measuring distances in the desert north of Baghdad.'","o":["(ii)","(i)","(iii)","(iv)"],"k":0,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] They = the team of scholars commissioned by al-Ma'mun; must follow that sentence.","h":"بعد ذكر فريق المأمون."},{"n":3,"s":"According to paragraph 2, how did Eratosthenes estimate the Earth's circumference?","o":["by sailing around the world","by watching eclipses of the Moon","by measuring the shadow of a ship","by comparing the angle of the Sun's rays in two cities"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] comparing the angle of the Sun's rays in two Egyptian cities.","h":"الطريقة."},{"n":4,"s":"The word commissioned in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["prevented","criticized","officially asked and paid someone to do","copied"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] al-Ma'mun commissioned a team of scholars.","h":"كلّف."},{"n":5,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that al-Idrisi's work was …","o":["forgotten soon after it was made","highly respected and influential","based only on Greek maps","limited to Sicily"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] used and copied for centuries.","h":"مؤثر."},{"n":6,"s":"The main purpose of paragraph 4 is …","o":["to describe the earliest maps","to describe a solution to a navigation problem and its drawback","to explain how satellites changed maps","to praise Ptolemy"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Mercator solved this … However, his method greatly enlarged areas.","h":"حل وعيب."},{"n":7,"s":"Why does the author compare Greenland and Africa?","o":["to show which continent is colder","to describe the Age of Exploration","to explain how compasses work","to illustrate the distortion of the Mercator projection"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] Greenland appears almost as large as Africa, although Africa is fourteen times bigger.","h":"توضيح التشويه."},{"n":8,"s":"The word revolutionized in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["slowed down","changed completely","made more expensive","copied"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] satellites revolutionized mapmaking once again.","h":"أحدث ثورة."},{"n":9,"s":"What is the function of the final paragraph?","o":["to bring the history to the present and link it back to the beginning","to introduce a new historical period","to give another example of Islamic maps","to argue that digital maps are useless"],"k":0,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] modern maps share something with the Babylonian clay tablet.","h":"الخاتمة والربط."},{"n":10,"s":"The author's main point in the last sentence is that maps …","o":["always reflect the choices and priorities of their makers","are perfectly accurate today","were more honest in ancient times","should not be used for travel"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] they reflect … the priorities of the people who make them.","h":"الفكرة."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The science of earthquakes","(469 words)","[1] Every year, the Earth experiences hundreds of thousands of earthquakes. Most are too weak to be felt, but a few release enough energy to destroy cities. Understanding why earthquakes happen, and where they are most likely to occur, has become one of the most important tasks of modern geology. The stakes are high: in the past century, earthquakes and the tsunamis they triggered have killed well over a million people.","[2] The explanation lies in plate tectonics. The Earth's outer shell, the lithosphere, is broken into about fifteen large plates and many smaller ones, which move slowly — a few centimetres a year, about the speed at which fingernails grow. Where plates meet, they may collide, pull apart or slide past each other. Because the edges are rough, the plates often lock together while the forces behind them continue to build up. When the stress finally exceeds the strength of the rocks, they break and slip suddenly, sending out waves of energy. This sudden release is an earthquake.","[3] The energy travels through the Earth as seismic waves. (i) Primary waves, or P-waves, are the fastest; they compress and expand the rock in the direction they travel, and they can pass through both solids and liquids. (ii) Secondary waves, or S-waves, move more slowly and shake the ground from side to side. (iii) Surface waves arrive last but often cause the most damage because they produce the largest ground movements. (iv)","[4] Scientists measure earthquakes in two different ways. Magnitude describes the energy released at the source and is usually expressed on the moment magnitude scale. Each whole number on the scale represents about thirty-two times more energy than the one below it, so a magnitude 7 earthquake releases roughly a thousand times more energy than a magnitude 5. Intensity, by contrast, describes how strongly the shaking is felt at a particular location, which depends on distance, depth and local soil conditions. Soft, wet soil, for example, can greatly amplify shaking, which is why buildings on reclaimed land are often badly damaged.","[5] Despite major advances, scientists still cannot predict exactly when a large earthquake will strike. What they can do is identify areas at high risk and estimate the probability of a major earthquake over decades. This information shapes building codes, which require structures in dangerous zones to bend rather than collapse. Early warning systems, such as those in Japan and Mexico, detect the first P-waves and send alerts that may give people a few seconds to take cover before the stronger shaking arrives.","[6] Saudi Arabia is generally less exposed than countries such as Japan or Türkiye, but the western regions near the Red Sea, where the African and Arabian plates are slowly separating, do experience earthquakes. The Saudi Geological Survey operates a national network of seismic stations to monitor this activity."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 2?","o":["to describe types of seismic waves","to explain the cause of earthquakes","to give examples of famous earthquakes","to discuss early warning systems"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] The explanation lies in plate tectonics … This sudden release is an earthquake.","h":"السبب."},{"n":12,"s":"Why does the author compare the movement of plates with the growth of fingernails?","o":["to show that plates are made of the same material","to explain how rocks break","to compare animals and rocks","to help readers imagine how slowly plates move"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] a few centimetres a year, about the speed at which fingernails grow.","h":"التوضيح."},{"n":13,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence When the stress finally exceeds the strength of the rocks, they break and slip suddenly?","o":["Once the force becomes greater than the rocks can withstand, they suddenly break and move.","The rocks become stronger as stress increases.","Stress slowly weakens until the rocks stop moving.","The rocks never break under stress."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] stress > strength → sudden break and slip.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":14,"s":"Where would the following sentence best fit in paragraph 3? 'Unlike P-waves, they cannot travel through liquids, which is how scientists discovered that the Earth's outer core is liquid.'","o":["(i)","(ii)","(iii)","(iv)"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] they = S-waves; must come right after the S-wave sentence.","h":"بعد الموجات الثانوية."},{"n":15,"s":"All of the following are true about P-waves EXCEPT …","o":["they are the fastest","they can pass through liquids","they arrive last","they compress and expand rock"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] P-waves arrive first; surface waves last.","h":"خطأ."},{"n":16,"s":"The main purpose of paragraph 4 is …","o":["to explain why earthquakes happen","to distinguish between two ways of measuring earthquakes","to describe damage in cities","to criticize scientists"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] magnitude … Intensity, by contrast …","h":"التمييز."},{"n":17,"s":"According to paragraph 4, a magnitude 7 earthquake releases about how much more energy than a magnitude 5?","o":["twice","thirty-two times","seven times","a thousand times"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] roughly a thousand times more energy.","h":"ألف مرة."},{"n":18,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that early warning systems work because …","o":["they can predict earthquakes days in advance","P-waves arrive before the more damaging waves","they stop surface waves","they measure intensity after the shaking"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] detect the first P-waves … before the stronger shaking arrives.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":19,"s":"The word collapse in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["fall down suddenly","bend slightly","stay strong","move slowly"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] bend rather than collapse.","h":"ينهار."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the function of the final paragraph?","o":["to summarize all types of waves","to argue that earthquakes cannot be measured","to describe Japan's warning system","to relate the topic to Saudi Arabia"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Saudi Arabia … western regions … Saudi Geological Survey.","h":"الربط بالسعودية."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): The origins of the Olympic Games","(461 words)","[1] The modern Olympic Games, with more than 10,000 athletes from over 200 nations, are the largest sporting event on Earth. Yet their name and many of their traditions come from a much older festival held in ancient Greece for over a thousand years. Understanding that festival helps explain some of the rituals and ideals that still surround the Olympics today, from the lighting of the flame to the idea of peaceful competition between nations.","[2] According to most historians, the ancient Olympic Games began in 776 BC at Olympia, a religious sanctuary in the western Peloponnese dedicated to Zeus, the king of the Greek gods. The Games were held every four years, and this four-year period, called an Olympiad, became a way of counting time in the Greek world. During the festival, a sacred truce was announced, allowing athletes and spectators to travel safely to Olympia even when Greek city-states were at war with each other.","[3] For the first decades, the Games consisted of a single event: a sprint of about 190 metres called the stadion. (i) Over time, more events were added, including longer running races, wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and the pentathlon, which combined running, jumping, discus, javelin and wrestling. (ii) Perhaps the most brutal event was the pankration, a mixture of boxing and wrestling with very few rules. (iii) Winners received no money at the Games themselves, only a crown of olive leaves cut from a sacred tree. (iv)","[4] Participation was limited. Only free Greek men could compete, and married women were forbidden even to watch the main events. Women had their own separate festival at Olympia, the Heraia, which included foot races for unmarried girls. Interestingly, women could win Olympic titles as owners of chariot teams, since the victory was awarded to the owner rather than the driver. Cynisca, a Spartan princess, did exactly this in 396 BC.","[5] The ancient Games continued under Roman rule but gradually lost their religious significance, and they were eventually abolished around AD 393. The idea was revived in the late nineteenth century by the French educator Pierre de Coubertin, who believed that international sport could promote peace and understanding between nations. The first modern Olympics took place in Athens in 1896, with just 241 athletes from 14 countries. Since then, the Games have grown enormously, and women have competed since 1900.","[6] Today's Games differ enormously from their ancient ancestor, yet the link remains visible. The Olympic flame is lit at Olympia using the rays of the Sun, and carried by relay to the host city. The marathon, although never part of the ancient Games, was created for 1896 to honour a Greek legend. Saudi Arabia has competed since 1972, and its athletes have won medals in equestrian events, athletics and taekwondo."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 1?","o":["to describe the pentathlon","to explain the sacred truce","to list Saudi medals","to connect the modern Games with their ancient origins"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] modern Games … traditions come from a much older festival.","h":"التقديم والربط."},{"n":22,"s":"The word truce in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["a sports competition","an agreement to stop fighting for a period","a religious building","a type of race"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] allowing safe travel even when city-states were at war.","h":"هدنة."},{"n":23,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that the Olympic Games were …","o":["important enough to influence how the Greeks measured time","held every year","only for soldiers","forbidden during wars"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] an Olympiad became a way of counting time.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":24,"s":"Where would the following sentence best fit in paragraph 3? 'However, they returned home as heroes and were often rewarded by their cities with money, free meals and statues.'","o":["(i)","(ii)","(iii)","(iv)"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] However contrasts with no money … only a crown; they = winners.","h":"بعد ذكر الجائزة."},{"n":25,"s":"All of the following were part of the pentathlon EXCEPT …","o":["running","jumping","boxing","wrestling"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] running, jumping, discus, javelin, wrestling; boxing ✗.","h":"ليس من الخماسي."},{"n":26,"s":"The main purpose of paragraph 4 is …","o":["to explain how the Games ended","to describe the events in detail","to describe who could and could not take part","to compare Greek and Roman sport"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Participation was limited …","h":"المشاركة."},{"n":27,"s":"Why does the author mention Cynisca?","o":["to show that women competed in races at Olympia","to criticize Spartan law","to describe the Heraia festival","to give an example of a woman who won an Olympic title"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] women could win titles as owners … Cynisca did exactly this.","h":"مثال."},{"n":28,"s":"The word abolished in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["expanded","moved","officially ended","celebrated"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] eventually abolished around AD 393.","h":"أُلغي."},{"n":29,"s":"According to paragraph 5, why did Coubertin revive the Games?","o":["He believed international sport could promote peace.","He wanted to honour Zeus.","He wanted to make money for Athens.","He was asked by the Roman emperor."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] believed that international sport could promote peace.","h":"السبب."},{"n":30,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in paragraph 6 as a link between the ancient and modern Games?","o":["the olive-leaf crown given to every modern winner","the Olympic flame lit at Olympia","the relay of the flame to the host city","the marathon created to honour a Greek legend"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Olive crown for modern winners not mentioned.","h":"غير مذكور."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"5.4","t":"Academic Vocabulary in Context","html":"<p><strong>Unit 5 · Lesson 5.4 · CEFR B2+ · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Determine the meaning of academic words from context.</p>\n<p>2. Use word parts and context together to confirm a meaning.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحدد الطالب معنى المفردات الأكاديمية من السياق.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يستخدم أجزاء الكلمة والسياق معًا لتأكيد المعنى.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>At B2+, STEP vocabulary questions often test academic words such as substantial, inherent, viable, mitigate, comprehensive, unprecedented and feasible. You may not know every word, but you can usually work out its meaning by combining two tools: CONTEXT and WORD PARTS.</p>\n<p>Context clues include definitions (that is, in other words), examples (such as, for instance), contrast (however, unlike, rather than) and the general logic of the sentence. Word parts help too: un-/in-/im- (not), re- (again), pre- (before), -able (can be), -ify/-ize (make), bio- (life), trans- (across), multi- (many).</p>\n<p>Remember that the correct option must fit the meaning in THIS sentence. Test each option by substituting it into the sentence; the right one keeps the meaning and the grammar.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ The word … in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …</p>\n<p>▸ As used in paragraph 4, the word … means …</p>\n<p>▸ The phrase … could best be replaced by …</p>\n<p>▸ Which word is the opposite of … as used in the passage?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في مستوى B2+ تختبر أسئلة المفردات كلمات أكاديمية مثل substantial وviable وmitigate وunprecedented، ويمكنك معرفة معناها بالجمع بين السياق وأجزاء الكلمة. فقرائن السياق تشمل التعريف والمثال والتضاد ومنطق الجملة، وأجزاء الكلمة مثل un- (لا) وre- (مرة أخرى) وpre- (قبل) و-able (قابل) و-ize (يجعل). ثم ضع البديل مكان الكلمة لتتأكد أنه يحافظ على المعنى والقواعد.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Read the whole sentence and the sentence before it.</p>\n<p>2. Identify the part of speech (noun, verb, adjective) — the answer must match it.</p>\n<p>3. Look for a definition, example or contrast clue.</p>\n<p>4. Break the word into parts: prefix + root + suffix.</p>\n<p>5. Predict a simple meaning in your own words, then match it with an option.</p>\n<p>6. Substitute the option into the sentence to confirm.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اقرأ الجملة كاملة والجملة السابقة، وحدد نوع الكلمة، وابحث عن قرينة تعريف أو مثال أو تضاد، وحلل أجزاء الكلمة، وتوقع معنى بسيطًا بكلماتك، ثم ضع البديل مكان الكلمة للتأكد.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ An option that matches a different meaning of the word.</p>\n<p>✗ An option with the wrong part of speech.</p>\n<p>✗ An option that is related to the topic but not to the word.</p>\n<p>✗ An option that is the opposite meaning (missed a contrast clue).</p>\n<p>✗ A word that looks or sounds similar (affect/effect, adapt/adopt).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Drought-Resistant Wheat</strong></p>\n<p><em>(58 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Researchers have developed a variety of wheat that is far more resilient to drought than traditional types. In field trials, its yields declined only marginally in dry years, whereas conventional wheat lost almost half of its harvest. Although the new variety is not yet commercially viable, because its seeds are expensive to produce, scientists believe costs will fall.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The word marginally is closest in meaning to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. slightly B. completely C. suddenly D. greatly</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Contrast: whereas conventional wheat lost almost half.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> So the new wheat lost only a small amount.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The word viable is closest in meaning to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. able to succeed in practice B. alive</p>\n<p>C. visible D. legal</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Clue: because its seeds are expensive → not yet practical for business.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> viable = workable, successful.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">whereas تدل على التضاد، فـmarginally تعني قليلًا؛ وسبب ارتفاع التكلفة يدل على أن viable تعني قابلًا للتطبيق تجاريًا.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 A · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Artificial intelligence in medical diagnosis","(465 words)","[1] In recent years, artificial intelligence (AI) has moved from science fiction into hospitals. Systems trained on millions of medical images can now detect signs of disease in X-rays, eye scans and skin photographs, sometimes as accurately as experienced specialists. Supporters believe AI could transform healthcare, particularly in regions where doctors are scarce. Critics, however, warn that enthusiasm has sometimes run ahead of the evidence, and that a system which performs well in a laboratory study may behave very differently in a busy hospital with real patients.","[2] Most diagnostic AI relies on a technique called deep learning. Rather than being programmed with explicit rules, the system is shown a vast number of labelled examples — for instance, chest X-rays marked as healthy or showing pneumonia. By analysing patterns in the data, it gradually learns to distinguish between the categories on its own. Once trained, it can examine a new image in seconds, a task that might take a human expert several minutes.","[3] The results have been striking. In one widely cited study, an AI system detected breast cancer in mammograms with fewer false alarms and fewer missed cases than radiologists working alone. Other systems can identify diabetic eye disease, a leading cause of blindness, from a single photograph of the retina, making screening feasible in rural clinics without eye specialists.","[4] Nevertheless, the technology has significant limitations. An AI system is only as reliable as the data used to train it. If the training images come mainly from one population, the system may perform poorly on patients from other ethnic groups or age ranges, potentially reinforcing existing inequalities in healthcare. Many systems also function as a black box: they produce an answer without explaining how they reached it, which makes it difficult for doctors to verify or challenge the result. Researchers are therefore working on explainable AI, which highlights the parts of an image that influenced its decision.","[5] There are also unresolved questions about responsibility. If an AI system misses a tumour, who is accountable — the hospital, the doctor who relied on it, or the company that designed it? Regulators in many countries are still developing frameworks to evaluate the safety and effectiveness of medical AI before it is widely deployed.","[6] For these reasons, most experts envisage AI as an assistant rather than a replacement. In this model, the system screens large numbers of images and highlights suspicious cases, while doctors make the final decision and communicate with patients. Used in this way, AI could reduce workloads, shorten waiting times and allow specialists to concentrate on the most complex cases. Saudi Arabia's health sector, which is investing heavily in digital health, has already begun trials of AI tools in radiology. Early reports suggest that radiologists value the tools most when they are fast, accurate and easy to override."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The word scarce in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["highly trained","in short supply","very expensive","frightened"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] regions where doctors are scarce → few doctors.","h":"نادر / قليل."},{"n":2,"s":"The word explicit in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["hidden","complicated","temporary","clearly stated"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Rather than being programmed with explicit rules … learns on its own.","h":"صريح."},{"n":3,"s":"The word distinguish in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["combine","ignore","tell the difference","describe"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] learns to distinguish between the categories.","h":"يميّز."},{"n":4,"s":"The word feasible in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["dangerous","possible and practical","compulsory","unnecessary"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] making screening feasible in rural clinics without specialists.","h":"ممكن عمليًا."},{"n":5,"s":"According to paragraph 3, why is the AI eye-disease system useful in rural clinics?","o":["It replaces all doctors.","It treats blindness directly.","It is cheaper than glasses.","It can identify disease from one photograph without an eye specialist."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] from a single photograph … without eye specialists.","h":"الفائدة."},{"n":6,"s":"The word reinforcing in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["strengthening","removing","measuring","reporting"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] potentially reinforcing existing inequalities.","h":"يعزّز."},{"n":7,"s":"The phrase black box in paragraph 4 refers to a system that …","o":["is painted black","records flight data","cannot produce answers","gives answers without showing how it reached them"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] produce an answer without explaining how they reached it.","h":"الصندوق الأسود."},{"n":8,"s":"The word accountable in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["countable","responsible","available","reliable"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] who is accountable — the hospital, the doctor, or the company?","h":"مسؤول."},{"n":9,"s":"The word envisage in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to …","o":["reject","remember","imagine as a future possibility","test"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] most experts envisage AI as an assistant.","h":"يتصوّر."},{"n":10,"s":"The author's attitude towards AI in diagnosis is best described as …","o":["completely opposed","positive about its potential but aware of its risks","uncritically enthusiastic","uninterested"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] striking results … Nevertheless, significant limitations … assistant.","h":"متوازن."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): Green hydrogen as a fuel","(451 words)","[1] As the world searches for ways to reduce carbon emissions, hydrogen has attracted growing attention as a fuel of the future. When hydrogen is used in a fuel cell or burned, its only significant by-product is water. In theory, it could power heavy industry, long-distance trucks, ships and even aircraft — sectors that are difficult to electrify with batteries alone. Hydrogen is also the most abundant element in the universe, although on Earth it is almost always found combined with other elements, especially in water.","[2] However, not all hydrogen is equally clean. Today, nearly all hydrogen is produced from natural gas or coal through processes that emit large quantities of carbon dioxide. This is often called grey hydrogen. If the carbon dioxide is captured and stored underground, the product is known as blue hydrogen. Green hydrogen, by contrast, is produced by electrolysis: an electric current splits water into hydrogen and oxygen, and the electricity comes entirely from renewable sources such as solar or wind power. Its production therefore emits virtually no greenhouse gases.","[3] The main obstacle is cost. Electrolysis requires substantial amounts of electricity, and green hydrogen currently costs two to three times more than grey hydrogen. Storing and transporting hydrogen is also challenging, because it is the lightest element and must be compressed to very high pressures, cooled to minus 253°C, or converted into other substances such as ammonia, which can be shipped more easily. Each of these steps requires additional energy and specialized equipment, adding further to the final price.","[4] Proponents argue that these costs will decline rapidly as renewable electricity becomes cheaper and electrolysers are manufactured on a larger scale, mirroring the dramatic fall in the price of solar panels over the past decade. Sceptics caution that hydrogen is inherently inefficient for some uses: converting electricity into hydrogen and then back into electricity loses more than half of the original energy, so for cars, using electricity directly in batteries is usually more sensible.","[5] Saudi Arabia, with abundant sunshine, strong winds and vast areas of land, is well positioned to become a major producer. At NEOM, a large plant is being built to produce green hydrogen, which will be converted into ammonia for export. If such projects succeed, countries that once exported oil could become exporters of clean fuels, helping to mitigate climate change while sustaining their economies. Several European and Asian countries have already signed agreements to import hydrogen-based fuels in the coming decades.","[6] Whether green hydrogen eventually fulfils its promise will depend on technological progress, government policy and international demand. What seems clear, however, is that it will not replace all fossil fuels, but it may play a crucial role in the sectors where alternatives are scarce."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"The phrase by-product in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["a secondary substance produced in a process","the main purpose of a process","a type of battery","a fuel cell"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] its only significant by-product is water.","h":"منتج ثانوي."},{"n":12,"s":"The word electrify in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["excite greatly","make dangerous","make something use electric power","measure electricity"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] -ify = make; sectors difficult to electrify with batteries.","h":"يكهرب / يحوّل للكهرباء."},{"n":13,"s":"The word virtually in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["online","never","partly","almost"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] emits virtually no greenhouse gases.","h":"تقريبًا."},{"n":14,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what makes hydrogen green?","o":["It is made from natural gas.","Its carbon dioxide is stored underground.","It is made by electrolysis using only renewable electricity.","It is coloured green."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] electricity comes entirely from renewable sources.","h":"الهيدروجين الأخضر."},{"n":15,"s":"The word substantial in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["small","solid","large","cheap"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] requires substantial amounts of electricity → large amounts.","h":"كبير."},{"n":16,"s":"The word Proponents in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["supporters","opponents","producers","scientists"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] contrasted with Sceptics → supporters.","h":"المؤيدون."},{"n":17,"s":"The word inherently in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["occasionally","illegally","recently","by its basic nature"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] hydrogen is inherently inefficient … loses more than half the energy.","h":"بطبيعته."},{"n":18,"s":"Why do sceptics say batteries are more sensible for cars?","o":["Hydrogen is too heavy for cars.","Batteries produce hydrogen.","Converting electricity to hydrogen and back wastes more than half the energy.","Cars cannot use electricity."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] loses more than half of the original energy.","h":"السبب."},{"n":19,"s":"The word mitigate in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["reduce the seriousness of","cause","measure","ignore"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] helping to mitigate climate change.","h":"يخفّف."},{"n":20,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the final paragraph?","o":["Green hydrogen will be important in some sectors but will not replace all fossil fuels.","Green hydrogen will soon replace all fuels.","Green hydrogen has no future.","Only Saudi Arabia will produce green hydrogen."],"k":0,"e":"[claim / evidence] will not replace all fossil fuels, but … crucial role in sectors where alternatives are scarce.","h":"الخلاصة."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Language extinction and revitalization","(452 words)","[1] There are roughly 7,000 languages spoken in the world today, but their distribution is extremely uneven. Just over twenty languages are spoken by more than half of the world's population, while thousands of others have only a few hundred or a few thousand speakers. Linguists estimate that, at the current rate, between a third and a half of the world's languages could disappear by the end of this century. Many of these languages have never been written down or recorded.","[2] A language becomes endangered when children stop learning it at home. This rarely happens suddenly. Typically, a community comes under pressure from a dominant language associated with education, employment and social prestige. Parents, hoping to give their children better opportunities, speak the dominant language to them. Within a few generations, only elderly people remain fluent, younger people may understand the language but cannot speak it, and when the last speakers die, the language becomes extinct.","[3] The loss is profound. Each language encodes a unique way of understanding the world: its vocabulary for plants, animals, landscapes and family relationships often contains knowledge accumulated over centuries. Some indigenous languages distinguish dozens of types of snow or sea ice; others contain detailed classifications of medicinal plants unknown to modern science. Oral traditions, songs and histories may vanish with the language that carries them. In this sense, the death of a language is a loss not only for its speakers but for all of humanity.","[4] Yet extinction is not inevitable. Revitalization efforts in several parts of the world show that endangered languages can be revived. Perhaps the most remarkable case is Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious texts and was then revived as an everyday spoken language in the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries. In New Zealand, Māori was in steep decline by the 1970s, but language nests — preschools where children are immersed in Māori — and Māori-medium schools have helped to increase the number of young speakers. In Wales, bilingual education and Welsh-language media have stabilized the number of speakers.","[5] Technology offers new tools. Linguists record elderly speakers on video, create online dictionaries and develop apps that allow learners to practise pronunciation. Social media gives speakers of small languages a space to use them in daily life. However, experts stress that technology alone is insufficient: a language survives only if a community uses it at home and passes it on to children.","[6] The Arabian Peninsula itself contains endangered languages. The Modern South Arabian languages, such as Mehri, Soqotri and Shehri, spoken in parts of Yemen, Oman and southern Saudi Arabia, have relatively few speakers and are mostly unwritten. Researchers are now documenting them before valuable knowledge is irretrievably lost."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"The word distribution in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["the sale of books","the history of languages","the number of countries","the way something is spread among people or places"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] distribution is extremely uneven: 20 languages vs thousands.","h":"التوزيع."},{"n":22,"s":"The word dominant in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["most powerful and influential","most ancient","most difficult","least popular"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] a dominant language associated with education, employment and prestige.","h":"مهيمن."},{"n":23,"s":"The word prestige in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["money","fear","difficulty","respect and high status"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] social prestige.","h":"المكانة."},{"n":24,"s":"The word profound in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["slight","very great and serious","temporary","obvious"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] The loss is profound → deep and serious.","h":"عميق."},{"n":25,"s":"The word encodes in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["destroys","translates","contains in a particular form","hides completely"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Each language encodes a unique way of understanding the world.","h":"يرمّز / يحتوي."},{"n":26,"s":"The word inevitable in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["certain to happen","impossible","unfortunate","unexpected"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Yet extinction is not inevitable … can be revived.","h":"حتمي."},{"n":27,"s":"The word immersed in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["tested on","completely surrounded by","punished for","separated from"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] preschools where children are immersed in Māori.","h":"منغمس."},{"n":28,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as revitalization examples EXCEPT …","o":["Hebrew","Māori in New Zealand","Latin in Italy","Welsh in Wales"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Hebrew ✓ Māori ✓ Welsh ✓; Latin ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":29,"s":"The word insufficient in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["not useful at all","not enough","very expensive","unclear"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] in- (not) + sufficient → not enough.","h":"غير كافٍ."},{"n":30,"s":"The word irretrievably in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to …","o":["quickly","in a way that cannot be recovered","partly","accidentally"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] ir- (not) + retrieve (get back) + -ably → cannot be recovered.","h":"بشكل لا يمكن استرداده."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"5.5","t":"Claims, Evidence and Counter-Claims","html":"<p><strong>Unit 5 · Lesson 5.5 · CEFR B2+ · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Identify claims, supporting evidence and counter-arguments.</p>\n<p>2. Decide which statement would strengthen or weaken a claim.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحدد الطالب الادعاءات والأدلة الداعمة والحجج المضادة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يقرر أي عبارة تقوّي الادعاء أو تضعفه.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Argumentative passages are built from three parts. A CLAIM is a position the writer wants readers to accept (City centres should ban private cars). EVIDENCE supports the claim: statistics, research findings, examples, expert opinions. A COUNTER-CLAIM is an opposing view, which the writer may then answer with a REBUTTAL.</p>\n<p>STEP asks: What is the author's main claim? / Which evidence does the author use to support …? / Which of the following, if true, would most strengthen / weaken the argument? To strengthen a claim, choose new information that makes the evidence more convincing or removes a doubt. To weaken it, choose information that shows the evidence is unreliable or that the conclusion does not follow.</p>\n<p>Distinguish carefully between the author's claim and claims the author reports in order to reject them (Critics argue that …).</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What is the author's main claim?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following does the author use as evidence for …?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following, if true, would most strengthen the author's argument?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following, if true, would most weaken the claim that …?</p>\n<p>▸ How does the author respond to the counter-argument in paragraph 4?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يتكون النص الجدلي من: الادعاء (موقف يريد الكاتب إقناع القارئ به)، والدليل (إحصاءات، أبحاث، أمثلة، آراء خبراء)، والادعاء المضاد (رأي معارض) الذي قد يرد عليه الكاتب بالتفنيد. ولتقوية الادعاء اختر معلومة تجعل الدليل أقوى أو تزيل الشك، ولإضعافه اختر معلومة تُظهر أن الدليل غير موثوق أو أن النتيجة لا تترتب عليه. وفرّق بين ادعاء الكاتب والادعاءات التي ينقلها ليرفضها.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Find the claim: usually in the first or last paragraph, often with should, must, it is clear that.</p>\n<p>2. List the evidence: numbers, studies, examples, experts.</p>\n<p>3. Find counter-claims: critics argue, opponents claim, some people believe … and the author's rebuttal (however, yet, this ignores …).</p>\n<p>4. For strengthen/weaken questions, restate the argument as Evidence → Conclusion, then test each option: does it make the link stronger or weaker?</p>\n<p>5. Ignore options that are about a different topic, even if they are true.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ابحث عن الادعاء في الفقرة الأولى أو الأخيرة غالبًا، واجمع الأدلة، وحدد الادعاءات المضادة وتفنيد الكاتب. وفي أسئلة التقوية والإضعاف أعد صياغة الحجة (دليل ← نتيجة) واختبر كل بديل: هل يقوّي الصلة أم يضعفها؟ وتجاهل البدائل التي تتحدث عن موضوع آخر.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Confusing a reported counter-claim with the author's own claim.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an option that repeats the argument instead of adding new support.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an option that is true but irrelevant to the argument.</p>\n<p>✗ Mixing up strengthen and weaken.</p>\n<p>✗ Treating a single example as strong statistical evidence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Homework in Primary School</strong></p>\n<p><em>(54 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Many parents assume that more homework means better learning. However, research suggests that, for primary-school children, homework has little effect on achievement. A review of more than sixty studies found almost no link between homework time and test scores before the age of eleven. Therefore, primary schools should limit homework to short reading activities.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. What is the author's main claim?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Parents should help with homework.</p>\n<p>B. Primary schools should limit homework to short reading.</p>\n<p>C. Homework improves test scores.</p>\n<p>D. Sixty studies were reviewed.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Therefore … should → the claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> D is evidence; C is the view rejected.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. Which of the following, if true, would most WEAKEN the argument?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Many parents dislike homework.</p>\n<p>B. A large new study finds that daily homework clearly raises primary pupils' scores.</p>\n<p>C. Reading is enjoyable.</p>\n<p>D. Secondary pupils do more homework.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The argument rests on no link before age eleven.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> New strong evidence of a link would weaken it.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الادعاء يأتي بعد Therefore، ويُضعفه دليل جديد قوي يناقض الدليل الذي اعتمد عليه.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 B","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Should city centres ban private cars?","(464 words)","[1] In cities around the world, a quiet revolution is changing the way people move. Oslo has removed most on-street parking from its city centre and replaced it with bicycle lanes, benches and small parks. Madrid restricted access for most private cars to a large central area. Paris has turned riverside motorways into pedestrian promenades. These cities have taken a bold step that many others should follow: banning or severely restricting private cars in their historic centres.","[2] The case rests on several kinds of evidence. The first is health. Traffic is a major source of air pollution, which the World Health Organization links to millions of premature deaths each year. After Madrid introduced its restrictions, nitrogen dioxide levels in the centre fell significantly within a year. Fewer cars also mean fewer road accidents; in Oslo, no pedestrians or cyclists were killed in traffic in 2019, a record for a city of its size.","[3] The second is economic. Shop owners frequently fear that removing cars will drive customers away. Yet studies in several European cities have found the opposite: people who arrive on foot or by bicycle tend to visit more often and, over a month, spend as much as or more than drivers. Car-free streets with more space for cafés and seating attract visitors who stay longer. In Oslo, the number of pedestrians in the centre rose noticeably after parking was removed.","[4] The third is the efficient use of space. A single car parked in a city centre occupies around twelve square metres for most of the day, while it carries on average little more than one person when it moves. The same space could hold several bicycles, a café table or a tree. In dense cities where land is extremely valuable, devoting so much of it to stationary vehicles is difficult to justify.","[5] Critics raise legitimate objections. They argue that car bans harm elderly and disabled people, who may depend on cars, and that they simply push traffic and pollution into surrounding neighbourhoods. These concerns deserve serious attention, but they are not reasons to reject the policy. Successful schemes provide exemptions for disabled drivers, delivery vehicles and emergency services, and they are introduced together with frequent, affordable public transport. Monitoring in Madrid found no significant increase in pollution on the boundary roads.","[6] Of course, a ban that works in compact European cities with long histories of public transport cannot simply be copied everywhere. Cities designed around the car, such as many in the Gulf and North America, would need to introduce changes gradually, beginning with pedestrian zones and improved transit. But the direction is clear: city centres are for people, not for parked cars. The cities that recognize this first are likely to become the most attractive places to live, work and visit."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the author's main claim?","o":["Oslo is the safest city in the world.","Shop owners should move outside city centres.","Cities should ban or strongly restrict private cars in their centres.","Public transport is too expensive."],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] a bold step that many others should follow.","h":"الادعاء الرئيس."},{"n":2,"s":"Which of the following does the author use as evidence for the health benefits?","o":["the number of cafés in Paris","the size of a parked car","the history of public transport","the fall in nitrogen dioxide levels in Madrid"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] nitrogen dioxide levels in the centre fell significantly.","h":"الدليل الصحي."},{"n":3,"s":"The word premature in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["sudden and violent","expected","painless","happening earlier than normal"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] millions of premature deaths.","h":"مبكر."},{"n":4,"s":"How does the author respond to shop owners' fears in paragraph 3?","o":["by citing studies showing that pedestrians and cyclists spend as much or more","by agreeing that shops will lose customers","by suggesting that shops close","by ignoring the fears"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Yet studies … found the opposite.","h":"الرد."},{"n":5,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most STRENGTHEN the author's economic argument?","o":["Most shop owners own cars.","Cafés are popular in Paris.","Car parks are expensive to build.","Shop sales in car-free streets in several cities rose after the changes."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] Direct evidence of better sales supports the economic claim.","h":"تقوية."},{"n":6,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 4?","o":["to describe the history of cars","to support the claim with an argument about the use of space","to present a counter-argument","to conclude the passage"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] The third is the efficient use of space.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":7,"s":"According to paragraph 5, what do critics claim?","o":["Car bans increase pollution in city centres.","Car bans harm elderly and disabled people and move traffic elsewhere.","Public transport is always crowded.","Bicycles are dangerous."],"k":1,"e":"[detail] harm elderly and disabled people … push traffic … into surrounding neighbourhoods.","h":"ادعاء المعارضين."},{"n":8,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most WEAKEN the author's response to critics?","o":["New data show that pollution rose sharply on roads surrounding car-free zones.","Disabled drivers use special permits.","Madrid has a metro system.","Some critics own shops."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] The author's rebuttal relies on no increase on boundary roads.","h":"إضعاف."},{"n":9,"s":"The word exemptions in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["punishments","new taxes","traffic lights","special permissions not to follow a rule"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] provide exemptions for disabled drivers, delivery vehicles …","h":"استثناءات."},{"n":10,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 6 that the author believes cities in the Gulf …","o":["should ban all cars immediately","should move towards car-free centres gradually","can never reduce car use","already have no cars in their centres"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] would need to introduce changes gradually … the direction is clear.","h":"استنتاج."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): Do video games improve thinking skills?","(452 words)","[1] Video games have long had a poor reputation among parents, teachers and many doctors. They have been blamed for everything from short attention spans and aggression to poor school results. In recent years, however, a growing body of research has led some scientists to make a surprising claim: certain types of video games may actually improve thinking skills. The claim matters because billions of people, including most teenagers, play video games regularly, and the global games industry is now larger than the film and music industries combined.","[2] The strongest evidence concerns action games, in which players must react quickly to fast-moving events. In a series of experiments, cognitive scientist Daphne Bavelier and her colleagues found that regular players of action games performed better than non-players on tests of visual attention — for example, tracking several moving objects at once or spotting a target among distractions. Crucially, when non-players were trained on action games for several weeks, their performance also improved, suggesting that the games caused the improvement rather than simply attracting people who already had these skills.","[3] Other studies have linked strategy and puzzle games to improvements in problem-solving and planning. Players of complex strategy games must manage resources, anticipate opponents' moves and adapt plans quickly. Some researchers argue that such games train flexible thinking in ways that traditional classroom tasks rarely do. A few schools have even begun to use carefully chosen games in lessons, although such programmes remain experimental.","[4] Not all researchers are convinced. Critics point out that many studies have small samples and that some striking results have not been replicated. A large analysis combining dozens of studies found that the benefits of brain-training games, in particular, were small and did not transfer to everyday tasks: people improved at the games themselves but not at school or work. Moreover, the skills improved by action games are quite specific; there is little evidence that gaming raises general intelligence or academic achievement.","[5] There are also concerns about time and content. Hours spent gaming may replace sleep, physical activity, reading or homework, which have well-established benefits. Some games contain violent content, and a small proportion of players develop problematic gaming habits, which the World Health Organization now recognizes as gaming disorder. Parents are often unsure where to draw the line between healthy enjoyment and harmful excess.","[6] The fairest conclusion, therefore, is a nuanced one. Some video games can sharpen particular mental skills, and they need not be seen as purely harmful. However, the benefits are narrower than enthusiastic headlines suggest, and they depend heavily on the type of game and the amount of time spent playing. Moderate gaming, as part of a balanced and active life, appears to be the most sensible approach."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What is the author's overall position on whether video games improve thinking?","o":["Video games always damage the brain.","Video games raise general intelligence.","Some games improve specific skills, but the benefits are limited.","Video games are better than school."],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] nuanced … can sharpen particular skills … benefits are narrower.","h":"الموقف العام."},{"n":12,"s":"Why does the author describe the training experiment as crucial?","o":["It was the largest study ever.","It suggests games cause the improvement rather than attracting skilled players.","It proved games raise school marks.","It showed games are violent."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] suggesting that the games caused the improvement rather than simply attracting people.","h":"أهمية التجربة."},{"n":13,"s":"The word anticipate in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["copy","forget","predict and prepare for","attack"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] anticipate opponents' moves.","h":"يتوقع."},{"n":14,"s":"Which of the following is used as evidence AGAINST the claim that games improve thinking?","o":["Bavelier's experiments on visual attention","studies of strategy games and planning","a large analysis showing brain-training benefits did not transfer to everyday tasks","the training of non-players for several weeks"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] benefits … were small and did not transfer.","h":"دليل مضاد."},{"n":15,"s":"The word replicated in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["repeated with the same results","published","criticized","invented"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] some striking results have not been replicated.","h":"تكرار النتائج."},{"n":16,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most STRENGTHEN the claim that action games improve attention?","o":["Many teenagers enjoy action games.","Action games are expensive.","A large new study confirms that non-players trained on action games improve their attention.","Some games are violent."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] Replicated causal evidence strengthens the claim.","h":"تقوية."},{"n":17,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most WEAKEN the critics' point about transfer?","o":["Students who played strategy games improved their maths problem-solving at school.","Brain-training games are popular.","Some studies have small samples.","Gaming disorder is recognized by the WHO."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Evidence of transfer to school tasks weakens the critics' point.","h":"إضعاف."},{"n":18,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 5?","o":["to present evidence for the benefits of games","to describe how games are designed","to conclude the passage","to raise additional concerns about gaming beyond the research on thinking skills"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] There are also concerns about time and content.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":19,"s":"The word nuanced in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to …","o":["extreme","showing small but important differences","simple","confused"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] The fairest conclusion is a nuanced one … some games, narrower benefits.","h":"دقيق / متوازن."},{"n":20,"s":"The author would most likely advise parents to …","o":["ban all video games","allow moderate gaming as part of a balanced routine","encourage children to play as much as possible","choose only brain-training games"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] Moderate gaming, as part of a balanced life.","h":"النصيحة."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Nuclear power and climate goals","(467 words)","[1] Few energy sources divide opinion as sharply as nuclear power. For its supporters, it is an essential tool in the fight against climate change; for its opponents, it is a dangerous and expensive distraction. As countries attempt to reach net-zero emissions by the middle of the century, this old debate has taken on new urgency. Governments must decide now, because nuclear plants take many years to build and then operate for half a century or more.","[2] Supporters begin with emissions. Over their full life cycle, nuclear power plants produce about as little carbon dioxide per unit of electricity as wind power and less than solar. Unlike wind and solar, they generate electricity continuously, regardless of weather or time of day, providing a stable foundation for the electricity grid. France, which produces around seventy percent of its electricity from nuclear energy, has one of the lowest carbon intensities of any large economy in Europe. A single large reactor can supply electricity to millions of homes while occupying a relatively small area of land.","[3] Supporters also argue that nuclear power is far safer than its reputation suggests. Measured by deaths per unit of electricity produced, including accidents and air pollution, nuclear energy ranks among the safest sources, far safer than coal, oil or gas. Even including Chernobyl and Fukushima, the total number of deaths directly attributed to nuclear power is small compared with the millions caused each year by air pollution from fossil fuels.","[4] Opponents raise several objections. The first is cost and time. Recent nuclear projects in Europe and the United States have run years behind schedule and billions of dollars over budget. Critics argue that the same money invested in solar, wind and storage would cut emissions faster. The second is waste: spent nuclear fuel remains highly radioactive for thousands of years, and very few countries have opened permanent disposal sites. The third is public fear, which, whether or not it is justified, makes new plants politically difficult to approve.","[5] Supporters reply that cost overruns reflect a lack of recent experience rather than an inherent problem, pointing to countries such as South Korea and the UAE, whose Barakah plant was built relatively close to schedule. They also note that the volume of nuclear waste is small and can be stored safely. New designs, known as small modular reactors, promise to be cheaper and quicker to build, although none has yet operated commercially at scale.","[6] Ultimately, the question is not whether nuclear power is perfect, but whether the world can meet its climate goals without it. Many energy experts, including the International Energy Agency, conclude that reaching net zero will be harder and more expensive if nuclear capacity declines. The debate is therefore likely to depend on whether new projects can be delivered on time and on budget."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","o":["to explain how nuclear reactors work","to argue that nuclear power must be banned","to describe the Chernobyl accident","to present arguments for and against nuclear power in relation to climate goals"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] Supporters … Opponents … Supporters reply … Ultimately.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":22,"s":"Which of the following do supporters use as evidence that nuclear power is low-carbon?","o":["France's low carbon intensity with seventy percent nuclear electricity","the delays in European projects","the radioactivity of spent fuel","public fear of accidents"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] France … one of the lowest carbon intensities.","h":"الدليل."},{"n":23,"s":"The word continuously in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["occasionally","cheaply","without stopping","quietly"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] generate electricity continuously, regardless of weather.","h":"باستمرار."},{"n":24,"s":"Which of the following best restates the claim in paragraph 3?","o":["When all deaths per unit of energy are counted, nuclear power is one of the safest sources.","Nuclear power has caused more deaths than coal.","Nuclear accidents have never killed anyone.","Air pollution comes mainly from nuclear plants."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] ranks among the safest sources … far safer than coal, oil or gas.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":25,"s":"All of the following are objections raised by opponents EXCEPT …","o":["projects are often late and over budget","nuclear plants produce large amounts of carbon dioxide","waste stays radioactive for thousands of years","public fear makes approval difficult"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] cost ✓ waste ✓ fear ✓; high CO2 ✗ (supporters say it is low).","h":"ليس اعتراضًا."},{"n":26,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most WEAKEN the opponents' cost argument?","o":["Solar panels become more efficient.","Public fear of nuclear power increases.","Several new nuclear plants are completed on time and at the planned cost.","More waste is produced."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] Opponents rely on overruns; on-time projects weaken this.","h":"إضعاف."},{"n":27,"s":"How do supporters respond to the problem of cost overruns?","o":["They admit nuclear power is too expensive.","They say it reflects inexperience, citing examples like Barakah.","They argue that cost is not important.","They suggest building coal plants instead."],"k":1,"e":"[detail] reflect a lack of recent experience … Barakah plant.","h":"الرد."},{"n":28,"s":"The word inherent in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["basic and permanent","temporary","external","unusual"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] a lack of recent experience rather than an inherent problem.","h":"متأصل."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most STRENGTHEN the conclusion in paragraph 6?","o":["Nuclear plants are large buildings.","Some people fear radiation.","France also uses hydropower.","Models show that net zero costs much more in scenarios without nuclear power."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] Supports harder and more expensive without nuclear.","h":"تقوية."},{"n":30,"s":"The author's own position in the final paragraph is best described as …","o":["cautiously supportive of a role for nuclear power","strongly opposed to nuclear power","certain that nuclear power is perfect","uninterested in climate goals"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] not whether perfect, but whether goals can be met without it … depends on delivery.","h":"مؤيد بحذر."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"5.6","t":"Timed STEP Reading: Speed and Accuracy","html":"<p><strong>Unit 5 · Lesson 5.6 · CEFR B2+ · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Apply a time plan of about one minute per question.</p>\n<p>2. Prioritize questions and avoid time traps.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يطبّق الطالب خطة زمنية بمعدل دقيقة تقريبًا لكل سؤال.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يرتّب أولويات الأسئلة ويتجنب فخاخ الوقت.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>In the STEP Reading section, time is as important as understanding. A useful plan for a long passage with ten questions is: about two minutes to skim and map the passage, then about one minute per question, leaving a minute or two to review. That is roughly thirteen to fifteen minutes per passage.</p>\n<p>Some questions are fast: vocabulary, reference and direct detail questions usually take less than a minute. Others are slow: NOT/EXCEPT, paraphrase, inference and summary questions often need more time. Answer fast questions first, mark slow ones, and return to them.</p>\n<p>Avoid the three main time traps: rereading the whole passage for every question; spending more than two minutes on one question; and changing correct answers without a clear reason. Never leave a question unanswered — make your best choice and move on.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ (Mixed STEP set — all question types)</p>\n<p>▸ Main idea / best title</p>\n<p>▸ Vocabulary in context</p>\n<p>▸ Reference</p>\n<p>▸ Detail and NOT/EXCEPT</p>\n<p>▸ Inference, purpose and summary</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في قسم القراءة الوقت مهم كالفهم. وخطة مفيدة للنص الطويل ذي العشرة أسئلة: دقيقتان للمسح ورسم الخريطة، ثم دقيقة لكل سؤال، ودقيقة أو اثنتان للمراجعة، أي ثلاث عشرة إلى خمس عشرة دقيقة للنص. أجب عن الأسئلة السريعة (المفردات، الإحالة، التفاصيل) أولًا، وأجّل البطيئة (NOT، إعادة الصياغة، الاستنتاج، الملخص)، وتجنب إعادة قراءة النص كله لكل سؤال، ولا تتجاوز دقيقتين في سؤال واحد، ولا تترك سؤالًا بلا إجابة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Start a timer: 2 minutes for skimming and mapping.</p>\n<p>2. First pass: answer quick questions (vocabulary, reference, direct detail) in under a minute each.</p>\n<p>3. Mark slow questions (NOT, paraphrase, inference, summary) and return to them.</p>\n<p>4. If you are still unsure after two minutes, eliminate what you can, choose, and move on.</p>\n<p>5. Use the last minute to check marked questions — change an answer only with clear evidence.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ابدأ بمؤقت: دقيقتان للمسح، ثم أجب عن الأسئلة السريعة في أقل من دقيقة لكل منها، وضع علامة على الأسئلة البطيئة لتعود إليها، وإن بقيت مترددًا بعد دقيقتين فاستبعد ما تستطيع واختر وانتقل، واستخدم الدقيقة الأخيرة للمراجعة ولا تغيّر إجابة إلا بدليل واضح.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Rereading the whole passage for each question.</p>\n<p>✗ Spending three or four minutes on one difficult item.</p>\n<p>✗ Leaving answers blank.</p>\n<p>✗ Changing a correct answer because of anxiety, not evidence.</p>\n<p>✗ Doing the questions strictly in order and running out of time on easy ones at the end.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Time Plan for One Passage</strong></p>\n<p><em>(45 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Minute 0–2: skim and write a paragraph map. Minutes 2–8: answer the quick questions (vocabulary, reference, detail). Minutes 8–13: return to the slow questions (NOT/EXCEPT, inference, paraphrase, summary). Minutes 13–14: check marked answers and make sure no question is blank.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. A student has spent two and a half minutes on a paraphrase question and is still unsure. What should she do?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Reread the whole passage from the beginning.</p>\n<p>B. Eliminate the clearly wrong options, choose the best remaining one, mark it and move on.</p>\n<p>C. Leave it blank.</p>\n<p>D. Change all her previous answers.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The time limit for one question has passed.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Eliminate, choose, mark, move on.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. Which question type should usually be answered in the first pass?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. a summary question B. a NOT/EXCEPT question</p>\n<p>C. a vocabulary question D. a paraphrase question</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Vocabulary questions are usually fast.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is C.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بعد دقيقتين في سؤال واحد: استبعد واختر وضع علامة وانتقل، وأجب عن أسئلة المفردات في الجولة الأولى.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 C","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Gut bacteria and human health","(464 words)","[1] The human body is home to trillions of microorganisms, most of them bacteria living in the large intestine. For a long time, these microbes were regarded mainly as passengers or, at worst, as sources of infection. Over the past two decades, however, research on the gut microbiome — the community of microbes in the digestive system — has transformed this view. Scientists now believe these organisms play an important role in digestion, immunity and possibly even mood. Some researchers have even described the microbiome as a forgotten organ, because its combined activity is so important to the body.","[2] One of their best-established functions is helping us digest food. Human enzymes cannot break down many types of dietary fibre found in vegetables, fruits, beans and whole grains. Gut bacteria ferment this fibre and produce short-chain fatty acids, which provide energy for the cells lining the colon and help to reduce inflammation. A diet low in fibre may therefore starve beneficial bacteria and allow less helpful species to dominate. Studies comparing people in different countries have found that those who eat traditional, plant-rich diets tend to have more diverse gut bacteria than those who eat mostly processed food.","[3] The microbiome also trains the immune system. Experiments with mice raised in completely sterile conditions, without any microbes, show that their immune systems develop abnormally. In humans, researchers have observed that children born by caesarean section or treated with many courses of antibiotics early in life have slightly different microbiomes, and some studies associate these differences with a higher risk of allergies and asthma, although the causes are not fully understood.","[4] Perhaps the most intriguing research concerns the so-called gut–brain axis. The gut and the brain communicate through nerves, hormones and immune signals, and gut bacteria produce chemicals that can influence this communication. In experiments, transferring gut bacteria from anxious mice to calm mice made the calm mice more anxious. However, evidence in humans is still limited, and scientists caution against assuming that changing one's diet or taking supplements can treat mental-health conditions.","[5] The commercial interest in the microbiome has grown faster than the science. Shops are full of probiotic products claiming to improve gut health, but the effects of many of them are uncertain, partly because different bacterial strains behave differently and because each person's microbiome is unique. Some well-designed trials have shown benefits for specific conditions, such as certain types of diarrhoea, but for most healthy people, the evidence remains weak.","[6] For now, the most reliable advice is also the simplest. A varied diet rich in fibre from plants, together with limited use of antibiotics when they are not needed, appears to support a diverse and healthy microbiome. As one researcher put it, the best way to look after your gut bacteria is to feed them well."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The passage is mainly concerned with …","o":["the history of antibiotics","how gut bacteria affect human health and what is still uncertain","how to choose probiotic products","experiments on anxious mice"],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] Map: new view → digestion → immunity → gut–brain → commerce → advice.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":2,"s":"The word regarded in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["avoided","measured","attacked","considered"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] these microbes were regarded mainly as passengers.","h":"يُعتبر."},{"n":3,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what do gut bacteria produce from fibre?","o":["human enzymes","vitamins only","short-chain fatty acids","antibiotics"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] ferment this fibre and produce short-chain fatty acids.","h":"الأحماض الدهنية."},{"n":4,"s":"The word this in paragraph 2 (ferment this fibre) refers to …","o":["dietary fibre that human enzymes cannot break down","sugar","the colon","inflammation"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] cannot break down many types of dietary fibre … ferment this fibre.","h":"الإحالة."},{"n":5,"s":"Why does the author mention mice raised in sterile conditions?","o":["to show that microbes are needed for normal immune development","to describe how mice are cleaned","to prove that antibiotics are harmful","to compare mice and humans in size"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] their immune systems develop abnormally.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":6,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as factors that can affect the gut microbiome EXCEPT …","o":["birth by caesarean section","playing outdoors","many courses of antibiotics early in life","a diet low in fibre"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] caesarean ✓ antibiotics ✓ low-fibre diet ✓; outdoor play ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":7,"s":"The word intriguing in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very interesting","very dangerous","well established","boring"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Perhaps the most intriguing research …","h":"مثير للاهتمام."},{"n":8,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 4 that the author believes …","o":["diet can cure anxiety","the gut and brain do not communicate","results in mice do not prove the same effects in humans","supplements are proven treatments"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] evidence in humans is still limited … caution.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes paragraph 5?","o":["Probiotics cure all digestive problems.","Each person's microbiome is the same.","Probiotic products are popular, but evidence of their benefits is mostly weak.","Shops should stop selling food."],"k":2,"e":"[summary / conclusion] commercial interest grew faster than the science.","h":"ملخص الفقرة."},{"n":10,"s":"The author's attitude towards commercial probiotic claims is best described as …","o":["enthusiastic","indifferent","hostile and angry","sceptical"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] grown faster than the science … effects uncertain.","h":"متشكك."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The archaeology of Hegra (Mada'in Salih)","(459 words)","[1] In the desert of north-western Saudi Arabia, about twenty kilometres north of AlUla, lies Hegra, known in Arabic as Al-Hijr or Mada'in Salih. In 2008, it became the first site in the Kingdom to be inscribed on UNESCO's World Heritage List. Its most striking features are more than one hundred monumental tombs carved directly into sandstone outcrops, their decorated facades rising up to sixteen metres high. Scattered across a wide plain of golden sand, the tombs create one of the most dramatic archaeological landscapes in the Middle East.","[2] Hegra was the principal southern city of the Nabataean kingdom, whose capital was Petra in present-day Jordan. The Nabataeans were originally nomadic Arabs who grew wealthy by controlling the caravan routes that carried incense, spices and other luxury goods between southern Arabia and the Mediterranean world. Hegra flourished from around the first century BC to the first century AD, when it was an important stop on these routes.","[3] Unlike Petra, Hegra has been relatively well preserved, partly because it was less disturbed by later construction. Many tomb facades bear inscriptions in Nabataean script, the ancestor of modern Arabic writing. These inscriptions often record the name of the tomb's owner, the date of construction and legal warnings against unauthorized burial or sale of the tomb, sometimes threatening fines payable to the king. Such texts give historians rare insight into Nabataean society, law and family life. Some inscriptions show that women could own tombs in their own names, which suggests they held significant legal rights.","[4] For many years, research focused mainly on the tombs. Since 2002, however, a Franco-Saudi archaeological team has excavated the residential area of the city, revealing houses, wells, a defensive wall and evidence of agriculture. These discoveries show that Hegra was not merely a cemetery but a living town with a population that may have numbered several thousand. The team also found evidence of a Roman military presence after the Nabataean kingdom was annexed by Rome in AD 106.","[5] The city's water management was particularly impressive. Hegra receives very little rain, yet archaeologists have identified more than one hundred wells, some cut deep into the rock. Combined with careful farming, these wells allowed the community to grow crops and support caravans in an extremely dry environment. Analysis of seeds and plant remains suggests that the inhabitants cultivated date palms, cereals and cotton.","[6] Today, Hegra is at the centre of efforts to develop AlUla as a major cultural destination. Visitors can tour the tombs with trained guides, and the Royal Commission for AlUla has invested in conservation, research and visitor facilities. Archaeologists stress, however, that tourism must be carefully managed: the soft sandstone is vulnerable to erosion, and increased visitor numbers could damage the very monuments people come to see."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["The History of Petra","How Sandstone Is Formed","Hegra: A Nabataean City in the Saudi Desert","Roman Soldiers in Arabia"],"k":2,"e":"[best title] Covers location, history, inscriptions, excavations, water, tourism.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":12,"s":"The word inscribed in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["carved with a knife","destroyed","discovered","officially included in a list"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] inscribed on UNESCO's World Heritage List.","h":"أُدرج."},{"n":13,"s":"According to paragraph 2, how did the Nabataeans become wealthy?","o":["by mining gold","by controlling caravan trade routes","by farming wheat","by fighting the Romans"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] grew wealthy by controlling the caravan routes.","h":"مصدر الثروة."},{"n":14,"s":"Why is Hegra better preserved than Petra?","o":["It was built of harder stone.","It was never visited.","It was less disturbed by later construction.","It is under the sand."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] partly because it was less disturbed by later construction.","h":"السبب."},{"n":15,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as information found in tomb inscriptions EXCEPT …","o":["the owner's name","the cause of the owner's death","the date of construction","legal warnings"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] name ✓ date ✓ warnings ✓; cause of death ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":16,"s":"The phrase Such texts in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["modern Arabic books","UNESCO documents","the tomb inscriptions","Roman laws"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] inscriptions … Such texts give historians rare insight.","h":"الإحالة."},{"n":17,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 4?","o":["to describe the tomb facades","to explain water management","to show that recent excavations changed the understanding of Hegra","to discuss tourism"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] not merely a cemetery but a living town.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":18,"s":"The word annexed in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["abandoned","taken control of and added to another state","visited","defended"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] the Nabataean kingdom was annexed by Rome in AD 106.","h":"ضُمّ."},{"n":19,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that without its wells, Hegra …","o":["could not have supported a settled population","would have become a Roman city","would have had more rain","would have been a port"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] very little rain … wells allowed the community to grow crops.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":20,"s":"The author's attitude towards tourism at Hegra is best described as …","o":["supportive but concerned about protecting the site","completely opposed","uncritically enthusiastic","indifferent"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] invested … however, tourism must be carefully managed.","h":"مؤيد بحذر."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Vertical farming","(453 words)","[1] By 2050, the world will need to feed nearly ten billion people, most of them living in cities. At the same time, farmland is being lost to urban expansion, soil degradation and climate change, and agriculture already uses around seventy percent of the world's fresh water. One proposed answer is to bring farming indoors and stack it vertically. Supporters describe this as a revolution in food production; critics see it as an expensive solution looking for a problem.","[2] Vertical farms grow crops in layers inside buildings such as warehouses, shipping containers or purpose-built towers. Plants are usually grown hydroponically, with their roots in nutrient-rich water, or aeroponically, with roots suspended in air and sprayed with a fine mist. Instead of sunlight, they rely on LED lights tuned to the wavelengths plants use most efficiently. Temperature, humidity and carbon dioxide levels are controlled by computers, and sensors constantly monitor the health of every tray of plants.","[3] The advantages are considerable. Because water is recycled, vertical farms can use up to ninety-five percent less water than conventional fields. Crops can be grown all year round, unaffected by drought, frost or storms, and without pesticides, since pests are largely excluded. A vertical farm occupying a small area can produce yields many times greater than a field of the same size. Locating farms inside cities also reduces the distance food travels, so vegetables reach consumers fresher. Shorter supply chains also mean less food is wasted during transport and storage.","[4] Nevertheless, the model faces serious economic challenges. Lighting and climate control consume large amounts of electricity, and energy can account for a quarter or more of operating costs. Several well-funded vertical-farming companies in Europe and North America have gone bankrupt in recent years, unable to compete on price with field-grown produce. Moreover, the range of crops that can be grown profitably is narrow. Leafy greens, herbs and strawberries, which grow quickly and sell at high prices, are well suited; staple crops such as wheat, rice and maize are not, because they need much more space and light relative to their market value.","[5] For countries with scarce water and harsh climates, the calculation may be different. In the Gulf region, where most fresh vegetables are imported and water is expensive to produce, vertical farms may offer greater benefits than in temperate countries with plentiful rainfall. Several vertical farms now operate in Saudi Arabia and the UAE, and if they are powered by increasingly cheap solar energy, their economics could improve considerably.","[6] Vertical farming, then, is very unlikely to replace traditional agriculture in the foreseeable future. Its realistic role is as a complement: producing high-value, perishable crops close to consumers, especially in places where water and arable land are limited."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Vertical farming will soon replace all traditional farms.","LED lights are cheaper than sunlight.","The Gulf imports most of its vegetables.","Vertical farming has important advantages but is economically suited only to certain crops and places."],"k":3,"e":"[main idea] Need → method → advantages → challenges → Gulf → complement.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":22,"s":"The word suspended in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["cut off","hanging","buried","frozen"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] roots suspended in air and sprayed with mist.","h":"معلّق."},{"n":23,"s":"According to paragraph 3, why can vertical farms grow crops without pesticides?","o":["Plants are genetically modified.","LED light kills insects.","Water contains pesticides.","Pests are largely kept out."],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect] since pests are largely excluded.","h":"السبب."},{"n":24,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as advantages of vertical farms EXCEPT …","o":["less water use","year-round production","fresher produce for city consumers","lower electricity use"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Electricity use is HIGH — a challenge.","h":"ليس ميزة."},{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Energy can account for a quarter or more of operating costs?","o":["Energy is a very small part of the costs.","Energy may make up at least twenty-five percent of the costs of running the farm.","Energy costs four times more than everything else.","Farms use a quarter of the world's energy."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] a quarter or more = at least 25%.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":26,"s":"Why does the author mention companies that have gone bankrupt?","o":["to criticize investors","to describe farming in Europe","to show that vertical farming faces real economic difficulties","to explain how LEDs work"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] unable to compete on price with field-grown produce.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":27,"s":"Why are staple crops such as wheat unsuitable for vertical farms?","o":["They need a lot of space and light compared with their market value.","They cannot grow in water.","They are not eaten in cities.","They are too expensive to sell."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] need much more space and light relative to their market value.","h":"السبب."},{"n":28,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that vertical farming may make more sense in the Gulf because …","o":["Gulf countries have plentiful rainfall","the Gulf has no cities","solar energy is not available there","water and imported vegetables there are expensive"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] most vegetables imported and water expensive to produce.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":29,"s":"The word perishable in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to …","o":["likely to go bad quickly","very expensive","easy to grow","imported"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] high-value, perishable crops close to consumers.","h":"سريع التلف."},{"n":30,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the passage?","o":["Conventional farming will disappear by 2050.","Vertical farming is too expensive to exist anywhere.","Only strawberries can be grown indoors.","Vertical farming will work best alongside, not instead of, conventional agriculture."],"k":3,"e":"[claim / evidence] Its realistic role is as a complement.","h":"الخلاصة."}]}],"n":30}],"sc":{},"review":{"quiz":[{"l":["Unit Review — 50 questions on new texts. Read each text and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","مراجعة الوحدة: خمسون سؤالًا على نصوص جديدة، اقرأ كل نص واختر الإجابة الصحيحة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): The basics of quantum computing","(469 words)","[1] Ordinary computers, from smartphones to supercomputers, process information as bits, each of which is either 0 or 1. Every photograph, message and calculation is ultimately a long sequence of these two values. Quantum computers work on a fundamentally different principle. Instead of bits, they use quantum bits, or qubits, which follow the strange rules of quantum physics that govern the behaviour of atoms and particles. These rules often seem to contradict everyday experience, which is one reason why quantum computing is so difficult to explain.","[2] Two quantum phenomena are central. The first is superposition: a qubit can exist in a combination of 0 and 1 at the same time, until it is measured. The second is entanglement: two or more qubits can become linked so that the state of one is connected to the state of the others, however far apart they are. By exploiting these properties, a quantum computer with enough reliable qubits could, in principle, explore an enormous number of possible solutions to certain problems simultaneously.","[3] This does not mean that quantum computers will simply be faster versions of ordinary ones. For most everyday tasks, such as browsing the internet or writing documents, they would offer no advantage. Their potential lies in specific types of problems. One is simulating molecules for drug discovery and materials science, a task that becomes impossibly complex for classical computers as molecules grow larger. Another is optimization — finding the best solution among countless possibilities, such as the most efficient delivery routes. A third, more worrying, application is breaking some of the encryption methods that currently protect online banking and communications.","[4] Building a practical quantum computer is extraordinarily difficult. Qubits are fragile: the slightest vibration, change in temperature or stray electromagnetic signal can destroy their quantum state, a problem known as decoherence. Many systems must be cooled to temperatures colder than outer space. As a result, today's machines make frequent errors, and researchers estimate that thousands or even millions of physical qubits may be needed to create a smaller number of stable, error-corrected logical qubits.","[5] In 2019, Google announced that its quantum processor had performed a specialized calculation in about three minutes that, it claimed, would take the world's fastest supercomputer thousands of years. Critics quickly argued that improved classical methods could solve the same problem far more quickly, and the task itself had no practical use. Nevertheless, the experiment was widely seen as an important milestone.","[6] Governments and companies are now investing billions of dollars in quantum research, and some countries are already developing new encryption methods designed to resist quantum attacks. Most experts believe that quantum computers capable of transforming industry are still years, perhaps decades, away. Whether that day comes sooner or later, it is clear that quantum technology will become one of the defining scientific challenges of this century."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The passage is mainly concerned with …","o":["how quantum computers work, what they could do and why they are hard to build","the history of smartphones","Google's business strategy","how to protect online banking"],"k":0,"e":"[main idea · 5.1] Map: bits vs qubits → phenomena → uses → difficulties → milestone → future.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":2,"s":"The word fundamentally in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["slightly","in a basic and complete way","temporarily","surprisingly"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] work on a fundamentally different principle.","h":"جذريًا."},{"n":3,"s":"According to paragraph 2, superposition means that a qubit …","o":["is always 0","is linked to a distant qubit","is colder than outer space","can be in a combination of 0 and 1 until it is measured"],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 5.1] can exist in a combination of 0 and 1 at the same time, until it is measured.","h":"التراكب."},{"n":4,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 3?","o":["to describe the history of computing","to explain which problems quantum computers could help with","to explain why qubits are fragile","to criticize Google"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function · 5.3] Their potential lies in specific types of problems.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":5,"s":"The word simultaneously in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["one after another","very slowly","at the same time","by accident"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] explore an enormous number of possible solutions simultaneously.","h":"في آن واحد."},{"n":6,"s":"The word their in paragraph 4 (destroy their quantum state) refers to …","o":["researchers","temperatures","qubits","machines"],"k":2,"e":"[reference · 5.6] Qubits are fragile … can destroy their quantum state.","h":"الإحالة."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most WEAKEN Google's 2019 claim?","o":["A classical computer solves the same calculation in a few days.","Google builds a larger quantum processor.","Quantum computers are expensive.","Supercomputers use a lot of electricity."],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 5.5] The claim depends on classical computers taking thousands of years.","h":"إضعاف."},{"n":8,"s":"Where would the following sentence best fit? 'For this reason, some of the most advanced machines are housed inside large refrigerators that look like golden chandeliers.'","o":["after the first sentence of paragraph 1","after the sentence about cooling systems in paragraph 4","after the sentence about entanglement in paragraph 2","at the end of paragraph 6"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function · 5.3] For this reason → must follow cooled to temperatures colder than outer space.","h":"الإدراج."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes the passage?","o":["Quantum computers use qubits to tackle certain complex problems, but technical challenges mean practical machines are still some way off.","Quantum computers will soon replace all ordinary computers.","Google built the fastest computer in the world in 2019.","Qubits are easy to build and very stable."],"k":0,"e":"[summary / conclusion · 5.2] Combines principle, uses, difficulties and future.","h":"أفضل ملخص."},{"n":10,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the passage?","o":["Quantum computers are useless.","Encryption is no longer necessary.","Quantum computing is promising but its full impact is uncertain in timing.","Practical quantum computers already exist everywhere."],"k":2,"e":"[claim / evidence · 5.2] years, perhaps decades, away … defining challenge.","h":"الخلاصة."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The placebo effect","(461 words)","[1] In medicine, a placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient — a sugar pill, an injection of salt water, or even a fake procedure. Logically, such treatments should have no effect. Yet for more than half a century, researchers have observed that patients who receive placebos often report real improvements in their symptoms. This puzzling phenomenon, known as the placebo effect, has become one of the most fascinating subjects in medical science, studied by doctors, psychologists and neuroscientists alike.","[2] The placebo effect is the reason modern clinical trials are designed as they are. When a new drug is tested, one group of patients receives the drug and another receives an identical-looking placebo. Ideally, neither patients nor doctors know who receives which, a method called double-blinding. Only if the drug performs significantly better than the placebo can researchers conclude that it truly works. Without this comparison, improvements caused by expectation, natural recovery or extra medical attention could easily be mistaken for the effect of the drug.","[3] The strength of the placebo effect varies widely. It is most powerful for conditions with a strong subjective component, such as pain, nausea, fatigue and anxiety. In some studies of chronic pain, a third or more of patients receiving placebos reported meaningful relief. Context matters too: larger pills, more expensive-looking packaging, injections rather than tablets, and a warm, confident doctor can all increase the effect. Surprisingly, some studies have found that placebos can help even when patients are told openly that they are taking a pill with no active ingredient.","[4] How can an inactive treatment produce real changes? Research suggests that expectation plays a central role. When people believe they will feel better, the brain can release natural painkillers called endorphins, as well as other chemicals that influence mood and perception. Brain-imaging studies have shown measurable changes in pain-related brain regions after patients receive placebos. Learning is also important: patients who have previously benefited from a real treatment may respond to a placebo that looks the same.","[5] There is also a negative counterpart, the nocebo effect. When patients expect harmful side effects, they often experience them, even from a placebo. In some trials, patients receiving placebos stopped treatment because of side effects they attributed to the pills. This suggests that the way doctors explain risks can influence how patients feel.","[6] The placebo effect raises difficult ethical questions. Deliberately giving patients fake treatments without their knowledge would undermine trust. However, many researchers argue that doctors can use the insights of placebo research ethically: by communicating with empathy, building positive expectations about effective treatments, and avoiding unnecessarily alarming descriptions of side effects. In this sense, the placebo effect is a reminder that healing involves not only chemistry but also the mind and the relationship between patient and doctor."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Sugar pills are the best treatment for pain.","Clinical trials are unnecessary.","Placebos can produce real effects through expectation, which has important implications for research and practice.","The nocebo effect is more powerful than the placebo effect."],"k":2,"e":"[main idea · 5.1] definition → trials → strength → mechanism → nocebo → ethics.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":12,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 2?","o":["to explain why clinical trials compare drugs with placebos","to give examples of placebo pills","to describe brain imaging","to discuss ethics"],"k":0,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function · 5.3] The placebo effect is the reason modern clinical trials are designed as they are.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":13,"s":"The word subjective in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["easy to measure","related to school subjects","very serious","based on personal feelings"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] conditions with a strong subjective component, such as pain, nausea, anxiety.","h":"ذاتي."},{"n":14,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as factors that can increase the placebo effect EXCEPT …","o":["larger pills","expensive-looking packaging","a warm, confident doctor","a cheaper price"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 5.6] larger ✓ packaging ✓ injections ✓ doctor ✓; cheaper ✗.","h":"ليس عاملًا."},{"n":15,"s":"Why does the author describe the result of open-label placebos as surprising?","o":["Patients improve even though they know the pill is inactive.","The pills were very expensive.","Doctors refused to give them.","No patients took part."],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 5.1] help even when patients are told openly …","h":"سبب الدهشة."},{"n":16,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most STRENGTHEN the claim that expectation causes placebo effects?","o":["Many patients prefer tablets to injections.","Blocking endorphins with a drug reduces the pain relief produced by placebos.","Placebos are cheaper than real drugs.","Some patients recover naturally."],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 5.5] Links placebo relief to the endorphin mechanism.","h":"تقوية."},{"n":17,"s":"The word counterpart in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["something that matches another but in an opposite or parallel way","a side effect","a type of pill","a doctor's assistant"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] a negative counterpart, the nocebo effect.","h":"نظير."},{"n":18,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes paragraph 5?","o":["The nocebo effect makes drugs work better.","Patients never stop treatments.","Side effects are always caused by real drugs.","Negative expectations can cause patients to feel side effects even from placebos."],"k":3,"e":"[summary / conclusion · 5.2] expect harmful side effects … experience them.","h":"ملخص الفقرة."},{"n":19,"s":"The author's attitude towards using placebo research in medicine is best described as …","o":["completely opposed","positive, as long as it is used ethically","indifferent","in favour of deceiving patients"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone · 5.6] can use the insights … ethically.","h":"إيجابي بشرط الأخلاق."},{"n":20,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the final paragraph?","o":["Good communication between doctor and patient can itself contribute to healing.","Doctors should give fake treatments secretly.","Chemistry has no role in healing.","Patients should avoid all side-effect information."],"k":0,"e":"[claim / evidence · 5.2] healing involves … the relationship between patient and doctor.","h":"الخلاصة."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Dark skies and light pollution","(481 words)","[1] For almost all of human history, the night sky was a source of wonder, guidance and knowledge. Travellers navigated by the stars, farmers timed their planting by the rising of certain constellations, and the Milky Way stretched visibly across the sky. Today, however, more than eighty percent of the world's population, and nearly everyone in Europe and North America, lives under skies polluted by artificial light. For about a third of humanity, the Milky Way is no longer visible at all. Many children growing up in large cities have never seen a truly dark sky full of stars, and some astronomers fear that an important part of human culture is quietly disappearing.","[2] Light pollution is caused by excessive, poorly directed or unnecessary artificial lighting. Streetlights that shine upwards, illuminated advertising, sports stadiums and brightly lit buildings all contribute to skyglow, the orange haze that hangs over cities at night. Unlike many forms of pollution, light pollution is increasing rapidly: a study using observations by thousands of volunteers found that the brightness of the night sky grew by around ten percent per year between 2011 and 2022.","[3] The consequences extend far beyond astronomy. Many animals depend on natural cycles of light and darkness. Newly hatched sea turtles find the ocean by moving towards the brightest horizon, which is naturally over the water; artificial lights on the coast can lead them inland, where they die. Migrating birds, which navigate partly by the stars, can become disoriented by brightly lit buildings, and millions are killed each year in collisions. Insects are attracted to lights in huge numbers, which may contribute to declines in insect populations that pollinate crops.","[4] Humans are affected too. Exposure to artificial light at night, especially blue-rich light from LEDs and screens, suppresses the production of melatonin, a hormone that regulates sleep. Researchers are investigating possible links between disrupted sleep cycles and health problems, although more evidence is needed.","[5] Unlike chemical pollution, light pollution can be reduced almost instantly. Solutions are often simple: shielding streetlights so that they point downwards, using warmer-coloured bulbs, installing motion sensors and turning off unnecessary lights late at night. Such measures also save energy and money. Lighting that is directed only where it is needed can also reduce glare for drivers, improving rather than reducing road safety. Several cities have reduced skyglow significantly while maintaining safety on their streets.","[6] Protected dark-sky places are also growing in number. These are areas where lighting is carefully controlled so that people can enjoy the natural night. In Saudi Arabia, AlUla's Gharameel area was recognized as an International Dark Sky Park in 2022, the first in the Gulf region, and it now attracts visitors for stargazing events. Such initiatives show that dark skies can be a cultural and economic resource rather than a forgotten luxury, reconnecting people with a view that inspired scientists, poets and travellers for thousands of years."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How to Navigate by the Stars","The History of Streetlights","Losing the Night: The Problem of Light Pollution","Sea Turtles of the Gulf"],"k":2,"e":"[best title · 5.1] Covers loss of dark skies, causes, effects, solutions, dark-sky parks.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":22,"s":"According to paragraph 2, by how much did night-sky brightness grow each year between 2011 and 2022?","o":["around eighty percent","around ten percent","around one-third","about two percent"],"k":1,"e":"[detail · 5.1] grew by around ten percent per year.","h":"النسبة."},{"n":23,"s":"The word excessive in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very old","more than is needed","colourful","hidden"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] excessive, poorly directed or unnecessary lighting.","h":"مفرط."},{"n":24,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 3?","o":["to describe the causes of skyglow","to explain melatonin","to describe the effects of light pollution on wildlife","to offer solutions"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function · 5.3] Many animals depend on natural cycles … turtles, birds, insects.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":25,"s":"The word disoriented in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very tired","attracted","frightened by noise","confused about direction"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] navigate partly by the stars, can become disoriented.","h":"مشوش الاتجاه."},{"n":26,"s":"All of the following animals are mentioned as harmed by artificial light EXCEPT …","o":["sea turtles","migrating birds","fish in rivers","insects"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 5.6] turtles ✓ birds ✓ insects ✓; fish ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":27,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most WEAKEN the claim that light pollution can be reduced almost instantly?","o":["Warm bulbs are popular.","Most streetlights cannot be shielded or replaced without years of expensive work.","Motion sensors save energy.","Some cities are very large."],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 5.5] The claim depends on quick, simple changes.","h":"إضعاف."},{"n":28,"s":"The author's attitude towards the evidence on human health is best described as …","o":["certain","dismissive","cautious","angry"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone · 5.6] Researchers are investigating possible links … more evidence is needed.","h":"حذر."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes the passage?","o":["Light pollution is growing fast and harms wildlife and possibly people, but simple measures and dark-sky areas can reduce it.","The Milky Way has disappeared forever.","Only astronomers are affected by artificial light.","AlUla has the darkest sky in the world."],"k":0,"e":"[summary / conclusion · 5.2] Combines growth, effects, solutions, dark-sky parks.","h":"أفضل ملخص."},{"n":30,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the final paragraph?","o":["Stargazing is only for scientists.","Dark-sky parks reduce tourism.","Dark skies can have cultural and economic value worth protecting.","Artificial light is unnecessary everywhere."],"k":2,"e":"[claim / evidence · 5.2] a cultural and economic resource rather than a forgotten luxury.","h":"الخلاصة."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 31–40): Animals shifting their ranges as climates warm","(471 words)","[1] As global temperatures rise, plants and animals around the world are on the move. Hundreds of studies have documented species shifting their geographical ranges towards the poles or to higher altitudes, where conditions remain within the limits they can tolerate. These movements are among the clearest biological signs that the climate is changing, and they are happening on every continent and in every ocean. A large analysis published in 2011 estimated that, on average, species were moving away from the equator at about seventeen kilometres per decade and uphill at about eleven metres per decade.","[2] Some of the clearest evidence comes from the oceans, where water temperature strongly influences where marine animals can live. In the North Sea, cod and other commercially important fish have shifted northwards and into deeper, cooler water over recent decades. Coral reef fish have been recorded in temperate waters off Australia and Japan, far from their traditional ranges. On land, butterflies in Europe and North America have been found farther north than ever recorded, and some mountain birds and plants are retreating towards summits.","[3] Range shifts are not simply a matter of species moving to a new address. Different species move at different speeds, which can break long-standing ecological relationships. A flowering plant may shift more slowly than the insects that pollinate it, or a predator may arrive in an area before its usual prey. In some cases, newly arriving species compete with or prey on local species that have no defences against them. The results can be unpredictable: some local species decline sharply, while others benefit from the new arrivals.","[4] Mountain species face a particular problem. As they move uphill, the area available to them shrinks, because mountains become narrower towards the top. Eventually, species already living near summits may have nowhere left to go, a situation some scientists describe as an escalator to extinction. Studies of tropical mountains, where many species have very narrow temperature tolerances, suggest that this risk is especially high there.","[5] Human activities complicate matters. Cities, farmland, roads and fences can block the routes that animals need to follow. For this reason, conservationists increasingly emphasize the importance of wildlife corridors — strips of natural habitat that connect protected areas and allow species to move. Some scientists have even proposed assisted migration: deliberately moving threatened species to new locations where the future climate is expected to suit them. Critics warn that this could create new invasive species and disrupt the ecosystems that receive them.","[6] The Arabian Peninsula, already one of the hottest regions on Earth, may see some of these changes too. Researchers are monitoring species in the cooler highlands of Asir, which provide refuge for plants and animals found nowhere else in the region. Protecting such areas, scientists argue, will be essential if the region's biodiversity is to survive a warmer future."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"The passage is mainly concerned with …","o":["the fishing industry in the North Sea","how and why species are changing where they live as the climate warms, and the problems this creates","the history of conservation","butterflies in Europe"],"k":1,"e":"[main idea · 5.1] Map: shifts → evidence → broken relationships → mountains → human barriers → Arabia.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":32,"s":"The word tolerate in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["prefer","avoid","measure","survive or put up with"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] conditions within the limits they can tolerate.","h":"يتحمّل."},{"n":33,"s":"According to paragraph 1, on average, how fast were species moving uphill?","o":["about eleven metres per decade","about seventeen kilometres per decade","about eleven kilometres per year","about seventeen metres per year"],"k":0,"e":"[detail · 5.1] uphill at about eleven metres per decade.","h":"السرعة."},{"n":34,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 2?","o":["to explain the causes of warming","to argue against conservation","to describe wildlife corridors","to provide examples of range shifts"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function · 5.3] Some of the clearest evidence … cod, reef fish, butterflies.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":35,"s":"Why does the author mention a flowering plant and its pollinating insects?","o":["to illustrate how different speeds of movement can break ecological relationships","to show that plants move faster than insects","to describe a new species","to explain photosynthesis"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose · 5.6] Different species move at different speeds, which can break relationships.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":36,"s":"The phrase escalator to extinction in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["a machine used by scientists","species being pushed uphill until no suitable habitat remains","a fast route to the summit","a new kind of mountain"],"k":1,"e":"[reference · 5.6] nowhere left to go … escalator to extinction.","h":"الإحالة."},{"n":37,"s":"The word deliberately in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["by accident","slowly","secretly","on purpose"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] deliberately moving threatened species.","h":"عمدًا."},{"n":38,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most STRENGTHEN the critics' concern about assisted migration?","o":["Wildlife corridors are popular.","Some species move uphill.","A species moved to a new area quickly spread and reduced local species.","Many scientists study butterflies."],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 5.5] Evidence of invasive impact supports the critics.","h":"تقوية."},{"n":39,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes paragraphs 3 to 5?","o":["Range shifts disrupt ecosystems, threaten mountain species and are complicated by human barriers.","All species move at the same speed.","Mountains become wider at the top.","Assisted migration has no risks."],"k":0,"e":"[summary / conclusion · 5.2] relationships, mountains, barriers and responses.","h":"ملخص الفقرات."},{"n":40,"s":"Which of the following can best be concluded from the final paragraph?","o":["Protecting cooler highland areas will be important for Arabian biodiversity.","Asir has no unique species.","The Arabian Peninsula will not be affected.","All species should be moved to Asir."],"k":0,"e":"[claim / evidence · 5.2] Protecting such areas … essential.","h":"الخلاصة."}]},{"l":["Text 5 (Questions 41–50): Astronomers of the medieval Arab world","(463 words)","[1] Between the eighth and fifteenth centuries, astronomers working in the Islamic world made some of the most important contributions to the history of science. Their work was driven partly by practical needs: determining the times of the five daily prayers, the direction of the qibla towards Makkah, and the beginning of the lunar months required precise observation and calculation. It was also inspired by a deep intellectual curiosity about the structure of the universe and by the rich inheritance of Greek, Persian and Indian scientific texts translated into Arabic.","[2] Baghdad's House of Wisdom, founded in the early ninth century, became a centre for this work. Scholars there translated Ptolemy's Almagest, the most important astronomical text of the ancient world, but they did not simply copy it. They tested its values against new observations and corrected many of them. Under Caliph al-Ma'mun, astronomers built observatories in Baghdad and Damascus and measured the length of a degree of latitude, producing a remarkably accurate estimate of the Earth's circumference.","[3] Later astronomers made further advances. Al-Battani, working in Raqqa around 900, calculated the length of the solar year to within a few minutes of the modern value, and his tables were used in Europe for centuries. Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi, in his Book of Fixed Stars of 964, described more than a thousand stars and recorded the first known observation of the Andromeda galaxy, which he called a small cloud. Many stars still carry names derived from Arabic, such as Aldebaran, Altair and Betelgeuse. These names are a lasting reminder of the period when Arabic was the leading international language of science.","[4] Astronomers in the Islamic world also improved scientific instruments. The astrolabe, a device that models the positions of the Sun and stars, was refined into a sophisticated tool used for telling time, surveying and finding the qibla. In the thirteenth century, the observatory at Maragha in present-day Iran brought together leading scholars, including Nasir al-Din al-Tusi, who developed mathematical techniques to describe planetary motion without some of the problematic features of Ptolemy's model.","[5] Historians have noted striking similarities between these techniques and models later used by Nicolaus Copernicus, whose sun-centred theory transformed European astronomy in the sixteenth century. Whether Copernicus learned of them directly or indirectly remains debated, but many scholars believe that knowledge from the Islamic world reached Europe through translations and contacts in Spain, Sicily and Byzantium.","[6] For many years, popular histories of science skipped from ancient Greece to the European Renaissance, treating the centuries in between as a dark age. Modern scholarship has shown that this view is mistaken. Astronomers of the medieval Islamic world preserved ancient learning, tested it critically and added original discoveries, forming an essential link in the long chain of scientific progress that continues in observatories and laboratories today."],"q":[{"n":41,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Ptolemy was the greatest astronomer.","Copernicus learned astronomy in Baghdad.","Astrolabes were invented in Europe.","Medieval Islamic astronomers made major, original contributions that linked ancient and modern science."],"k":3,"e":"[main idea · 5.1] Map: motives → Baghdad → advances → instruments → Copernicus → correcting history.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":42,"s":"According to paragraph 1, which practical need drove astronomical work?","o":["predicting the weather","building ships","determining prayer times and the qibla direction","measuring mountains"],"k":2,"e":"[detail · 5.1] times of the five daily prayers, the direction of the qibla.","h":"الحاجة العملية."},{"n":43,"s":"The word inheritance in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["money from a relative","knowledge received from earlier generations","a new invention","a religious duty"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] rich inheritance of Greek, Persian and Indian texts.","h":"الإرث."},{"n":44,"s":"Why does the author say that scholars did not simply copy the Almagest?","o":["to criticize their translations","to show that they disliked Ptolemy","to explain why the House of Wisdom closed","to emphasize that they tested and corrected it"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose · 5.6] They tested its values against new observations and corrected many.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":45,"s":"Where would the following sentence best fit? 'This faint patch of light, visible to the naked eye on dark nights, is now known to be a galaxy of hundreds of billions of stars.'","o":["after the first sentence of paragraph 2","after the sentence about the astrolabe in paragraph 4","at the end of paragraph 6","after the sentence about al-Sufi's observation of Andromeda in paragraph 3"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function · 5.3] This faint patch of light → the small cloud (Andromeda).","h":"الإدراج."},{"n":46,"s":"The word sophisticated in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["simple","ancient","advanced and complex","decorative"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 5.4] refined into a sophisticated tool used for many purposes.","h":"متطور."},{"n":47,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as uses of the astrolabe EXCEPT …","o":["telling time","measuring the depth of the sea","surveying","finding the qibla"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 5.6] time ✓ surveying ✓ qibla ✓; sea depth ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":48,"s":"Which of the following, if true, would most STRENGTHEN the view that Copernicus was influenced by astronomers of the Islamic world?","o":["Copernicus studied in Italy.","Al-Tusi lived in the thirteenth century.","The astrolabe was popular in Europe.","A Latin manuscript of al-Tusi's techniques is found in a library Copernicus used."],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 5.5] Direct evidence of access strengthens the influence claim.","h":"تقوية."},{"n":49,"s":"The author's attitude towards popular histories that skip the medieval Islamic period is …","o":["critical","supportive","neutral","amused"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone · 5.6] this view is mistaken.","h":"ناقد."},{"n":50,"s":"Which of the following best summarizes the passage?","o":["Arabic star names are the most important legacy.","The House of Wisdom only translated books.","Copernicus copied al-Battani's tables.","Driven by practical and intellectual motives, astronomers in the Islamic world corrected ancient work, made new discoveries and influenced later European science."],"k":3,"e":"[summary / conclusion · 5.2] Covers motives, corrections, discoveries, instruments and influence.","h":"أفضل ملخص."}]}],"n":50}};
